desc: Spectral Vowel Morpher - voice<->wash capture instrument
// =====================================================================
// Capture-based vocal instrument. Feeds on LIVE audio playing into it (drag a
// WAV onto the track, or send another track in -- the proper REAPER way).
// Capture a moment into a slot; it's analyzed two ways at once:
//   * harmonic (voice) -- pitch-detected additive resynth: keeps the vowel
//   * spectral (wash)   -- PaulStretch-style phase randomization: breathy bed
// TEXTURE crossfades between them (0 = voice, 100 = wash). Capture up to 8
// slots and MORPH between them. Captures persist across save/reopen.
//
// USE: put audio on the track. Input level up / Voice down -> you hear source.
// When you hear the moment, hit Capture. (Then sweep CAPTURE POINT by ear to
// land exactly on the vowel -- it re-analyzes live, compensating for the
// fraction of a second between hearing and pressing.) Pull Input down, Voice
// up, set Texture, and Morph between slots.
//
//   Capture point = where in the grab to analyze (0 = earliest .. 100 = at press).
//                   PER SLOT: each slot remembers its own point, so scrubbing one
//                   slot never retunes the others. Switching Capture slot shows
//                   that slot's saved point.
//   Texture       = 0 voice (harmonic) .. 100 wash (phase-randomized)
//   Wash grain   = short = rough/grainy, long = glassy/smooth (wash character)
//   Spread        = spectral blur (Hz);  Audition = monitor focused slot / morph
//   Auto-morph    = Off / Sweep (back+forth) / Glide once (first->last slot) / Shuffle (random-order wander)
//   Low / High cut = band-limit the resynth (High cut also thins the voice's
//                   partials, and sits at an absolute frequency so a Layer
//                   transposes underneath it rather than dragging it along).
//   Layers        = pitch-shifted copies of the SAME morph position -- an
//                   octave each way up to four, plus 3 free-interval Custom
//                   layers -- so an octave stack doesn't clash the way two
//                   instances on Shuffle do. Each level is a plain dB that
//                   means what it says; nothing rebalances behind you.
//   Drift         = suite-wide automation replacement: pick a parameter, and it
//                   wanders on its own (up/down amounts + a wall-clock period),
//                   so a mouse-free / screen-reader user gets evolving motion
//                   without drawing REAPER automation. All targets drift at once.
// =====================================================================

// --- Capture. Pick a slot, grab into it, then say where in the grab to look
//     and how much to smooth. Slots are numbered 1-8 to match how they are
//     spoken about; the engine is 0-based underneath and subtracts one. ---
slider1:1<0,8,1{All,Slot 1,Slot 2,Slot 3,Slot 4,Slot 5,Slot 6,Slot 7,Slot 8}>Capture slot
slider2:0<0,1,1{Off,Capture now}>Capture now
// Capture point and Capture average are NOT Drift or Ramp targets, deliberately
// (2026-09-11). Moving either re-analyses the captured sound -- a full FFT and pitch
// detection per slot -- which is heavy and lands as a click; a drift would do that
// over and over. Rozaya agreed, asking that the reason be written down.
slider3:0<0,100,1>Capture point (%, per slot: earliest .. at press)
// Capture average: how many analysis frames the WASH spectrum is averaged over.
// GLOBAL here, unlike Passage -- for the same reason Overtone below is. It first
// landed per slot only because it was ported wholesale from Passage, where per
// slot is the house rule; the pairing with Capture point that justified it does
// not hold up. Capture POINT has to be per slot: it indexes into that slot's own
// grab, so a shared value would be meaningless. This is a smoothness dial, not
// an index, and per slot it cost eight identical edits on every project.
// The loss is real but small: a slot that only needed 1 now takes the same
// time-smear as the slot that needed 6. In a field whose slots blur into one
// another that costs far less than it would on Passage's separate stops -- which
// is exactly why Passage keeps it per slot.
// 1 is the original single-frame behaviour and the default.
// 1-6 since 2026-09-13: the analysis never used more than CAPAVG_MAX frames, whatever the control
// said. Rozaya: "6 seems OK, just has needed, and not had, spread to compensate for it because
// back then we were capped." No saved copy was above 6 (135 read).
slider4:1<1,6,1>Capture average (frames, 1 = one frame)

// --- The morph. Which slot you are hearing, where between them you are, and
//     whether that position moves on its own. ---
slider5:1<0,1,1{Focused slot,Morph}>Audition
slider6:0<0,100,0.1>Morph (% across captured slots)
slider7:0<0,3,1{Off,Sweep (back+forth),Glide once,Shuffle (random)}>Auto-morph
// Rate Mode. Auto-morph time is a DURATION, and a duration's two honest units
// are seconds and beats -- so this is one switch, not a unit picker with a single
// entry, and not a ratio menu. Per R13 sync is not a unit. Per R11 there is no
// target selector either: R11 pairs one with "Every N beats" for rates expressed
// AS rates (Womb's heart is a BPM and needs somewhere to say how many beats one
// of its beats takes). A duration under sync is simply read as beats.
// Flipping it converts the value at the current tempo, so nothing changes
// audibly at the moment you switch -- only the unit you type in.
// A MODE ENUM, not an on/off. Rozaya, 2026-09-04: "I kinda hate that we have no
// rate mode... whoever built the host switch shouldn't have. I wish it'd been
// not used as an anchor, it's not one. bpm/seconds/hz/whatever belongs in a rate
// mode like everything else in the suite."
//
// Correct, and it was already written down -- the consistency plan's own audit
// lists this plugin as "a sync switch and no Every N beats. Half converted the
// other way." R13-revised settled the principle for the whole suite: synced-ness
// is an ENTRY IN THE UNIT LIST, not a competing boolean beside it.
//
// CALLED Rate Mode, like every other plugin. An earlier version of this comment
// called it "Rate Mode ... because this plugin has no rate", which was simply
// wrong on both counts, and Rozaya caught it: "what do you mean this has no
// rate. there's a rate mode at the top of the damn thing."
//
// There is a rate. `Auto-morph` picks its shape -- Sweep back and forth, Glide
// once, Shuffle -- and `Auto-morph time` is its period. A sweep going back and
// forth at a set period is an LFO in all but name.
//
// And the second half of the excuse was wrong too: three plugins in this suite
// already label their transport delay "Start delay (in rate mode units)", so a
// Rate Mode governing durations ALONGSIDE a rate is the existing convention, not
// something needing a new word.
//
// FREE TO CHANGE, and that is why it happened immediately: it was already a
// two-entry enum, so Off -> Seconds and On -> Beats kept the stored values 0 and
// 1 meaning exactly what they meant. No renumber, no migration, nothing touched.
//
// THE SUITE'S FOUR, 2026-09-04. Rozaya: "hz. independent bpm. host x. seconds
// ... look to how the rest of the suite handles this too. these things don't
// exist in isolation."
//
// Counted before choosing: the suite runs SIX different rate mode enums, so
// there was no single thing to copy. But the full set is always these four, in
// one of two orders -- {BPM,Seconds,Hz,Host x} in both Polyrhythms and Shepard
// Tone, {Hz,Seconds,BPM,Host x} in Tremolo and the Sweeping Filter. Took the
// three-plugin majority, which is also the order the two most-used plugins run.
// The remaining enums are two-entry ({Own BPM,Host x} and friends) in plugins
// that only ever had one unit; those are a different question.
//
// SECONDS NEEDS NO IMPLEMENTATION, which is Rozaya's point and worth keeping:
// Auto-morph time is natively a PERIOD, so Seconds is the identity. The other
// three are one line each -- BPM is 60/v, Hz is 1/v, Host x is v beats.
//
// DEFAULT IS 1 (Seconds), not 0, because Seconds is what this plugin has always
// meant and a default must not change anyone's sound. Every saved instance is
// migrated 0 -> 1 by tools/morpher_migrate_ratemode.py for the same reason.
//
// My invented {Seconds,Minutes,Beats} is gone. Minutes went with it: nothing
// ever stored it, and Ramp duration -- the control it was added for -- is in
// minutes everywhere in the suite and is not governed by Rate Mode anywhere.
// Value then mode since 2026-09-13, as R20 puts every rate block. Rozaya: "Yes".
slider8:20<0.01,1000,0.01>Auto-morph time (BPM / sec / Hz / beats per cycle / per beat)
slider9:1<0,4,1{BPM,Seconds,Hz,Every N beats,N per beat}>Rate Mode

// --- The sound of the morph. Everything from here to High cut shapes what the
//     captured spectra become; all of it is global, across every slot. ---
slider10:50<0,100,1>Texture (% wash)
slider11:150<5,1000,1>Wash grain (ms)
slider12:0<0,1000,0.1>Spread (Hz)
// --- The pitch block, 2026-09-13 (docs/layouts/spectral-vowel-morpher.md, THE PITCH LAYOUT):
//     Bubbler's and Passage's, for the whole plugin rather than per slot.
slider13:0<0,128,1{None,C-1,C#-1,D-1,D#-1,E-1,F-1,F#-1,G-1,G#-1,A-1,A#-1,B-1,C0,C#0,D0,D#0,E0,F0,F#0,G0,G#0,A0,A#0,B0,C1,C#1,D1,D#1,E1,F1,F#1,G1,G#1,A1,A#1,B1,C2,C#2,D2,D#2,E2,F2,F#2,G2,G#2,A2,A#2,B2,C3,C#3,D3,D#3,E3,F3,F#3,G3,G#3,A3,A#3,B3,C4,C#4,D4,D#4,E4,F4,F#4,G4,G#4,A4,A#4,B4,C5,C#5,D5,D#5,E5,F5,F#5,G5,G#5,A5,A#5,B5,C6,C#6,D6,D#6,E6,F6,F#6,G6,G#6,A6,A#6,B6,C7,C#7,D7,D#7,E7,F7,F#7,G7,G#7,A7,A#7,B7,C8,C#8,D8,D#8,E8,F8,F#8,G8,G#8,A8,A#8,B8,C9,C#9,D9,D#9,E9,F9,F#9,G9}>Source note (where zero is)
slider14:0<-1000,1000,0.001>Source fine tune (only with a Source note, in Semitones)
slider15:2<0,2,1{Hz,Semitones,Cents}>Source fine tune unit
slider16:60<0,127,1{C-1,C#-1,D-1,D#-1,E-1,F-1,F#-1,G-1,G#-1,A-1,A#-1,B-1,C0,C#0,D0,D#0,E0,F0,F#0,G0,G#0,A0,A#0,B0,C1,C#1,D1,D#1,E1,F1,F#1,G1,G#1,A1,A#1,B1,C2,C#2,D2,D#2,E2,F2,F#2,G2,G#2,A2,A#2,B2,C3,C#3,D3,D#3,E3,F3,F#3,G3,G#3,A3,A#3,B3,C4,C#4,D4,D#4,E4,F4,F#4,G4,G#4,A4,A#4,B4,C5,C#5,D5,D#5,E5,F5,F#5,G5,G#5,A5,A#5,B5,C6,C#6,D6,D#6,E6,F6,F#6,G6,G#6,A6,A#6,B6,C7,C#7,D7,D#7,E7,F7,F#7,G7,G#7,A7,A#7,B7,C8,C#8,D8,D#8,E8,F8,F#8,G8,G#8,A8,A#8,B8,C9,C#9,D9,D#9,E9,F9,F#9,G9}>Target note (only with a Source note, in Semitones)
slider17:0<-96,96,0.1>Transpose value (Hz / semitones / cents)
slider18:1<0,2,1{Hz,Semitones,Cents}>Transpose unit
slider19:0<-1000,1000,0.001>Fine tune
slider20:2<0,2,1{Hz,Semitones,Cents}>Fine tune unit
slider21:440<20,2000,0.1>Tuning reference (Hz)
// 0..100, and NOT wider -- settled by ear 2026-09-06.
//
// It was briefly widened to 0..400 because `nothing to fear.RPP` stores 300 here
// on two instances. Rozaya listened: *"above 100, it does nothing. which tells me
// the value was 1, fine and the slider is, 2, useless past 100"*. Both halves are
// right, and the code says why.
//
// The engine hard-clamps this to 100 (`eff_stereo_width` in @sample), so the wash
// width saturates there. `nothing to fear` runs at Texture 100 -- pure wash -- so
// the voice engine, the only other consumer, is silent in it. Nothing above 100
// could ever have been audible.
//
// So the 300 was a slip after all, most likely the Wash grain figure (also 300,
// in all three instances, where it belongs) typed into the wrong box. Narrowing
// back CLAMPS those two from 300 to 100, which is exactly what they already
// sounded like.
//
// THE RULE THIS ADDS TO THE RANGE SWEEP: a range that promises MORE than the DSP
// delivers is as misleading as one that stops too early. Widening is not
// automatically right -- the range should match what the code honours, and where
// the code clamps, the slider should say so.
slider22:50<0,100,1>Stereo width (%)
slider23:0<0,100,0.1>Denoise (%)
slider24:0<0,20000,1>Low cut (Hz)
// --- High cut. The mirror of Low cut, and the thing that makes an octave-up
//     Layer usable at all. Two deliberate differences from Low cut, both worth
//     knowing before you reach for it:
//       * it is applied in ABSOLUTE frequency, AFTER the pitch shift, where Low
//         cut is applied to the captured spectrum BEFORE it is resampled and so
//         slides with Pitch. A control whose job is "take the fizz off the top"
//         has to stay put while a layer transposes underneath it. Low cut keeps
//         its old placement so the saved projects sound exactly as they did.
//       * it shapes the VOICE as well as the wash -- partials fade out as they
//         cross it -- which makes the voice engine cheaper, not dearer.
//     20000 means off, and off is bit-identical to this control not existing.
slider25:20000<200,20000,10>High cut (Hz, 20000 = off)

// Overtone: bring one partial out far enough to be heard as a note of its own.
// GLOBAL here, unlike Passage -- this plugin is a field you sit inside, with one
// setting across the morph, rather than a route whose stops each have a character.
// Lifts the chosen partial and renormalises the bank by total power, so the
// output never gets louder. Harmonic 1 is exempt at any depth -- it is the drone.
// The HARMONIC is also a per-layer choice (see Layer overtone harmonic); lift and
// width stay global, because they are character and character can be uniform.
slider26:0<0,64,1>Overtone harmonic (0 = off, 1 = fundamental)
slider27:24<0,48,0.01>Overtone lift (dB the chosen harmonic rises by)
slider28:1<0.5,4,0.1>Overtone width (harmonics either side)

// --- Layers: extra copies of the WHOLE morph -- voice and wash both -- at a
//     fixed interval from the main pitch, locked to the same morph position.
//     That lock is the entire point. Two instances on Shuffle land on different
//     slots and CLASH; a Layer is the same capture an octave away, so octaves
//     stack consonantly and Shuffle stays usable. Layers ride Pitch (and its
//     Drift/Ramp) as offsets from it, so the stack transposes as one.
//
//     SIXTEEN layers, each with its OWN PITCH (2026-09-13, docs/layouts/spectral-vowel-
//     morpher.md, THE PITCH LAYOUT). Rozaya: "The layers were there to take away drift ...
//     I didn't mean for them to stay in lockstep. So we do need fine tune per layer, and we
//     do need all the modes that pitch has". The named-interval ladder is gone: Layer 1 is
//     the Original and can move like any layer; Layers 2-13 start on the ladder they
//     replace, 14-16 on the old Custom defaults. Every layer's pitch is an offset from
//     Transpose, so the stack still transposes as one.
//     All at 0, Polyrhythm v3's model: only a control actually moved reaches all sixteen.
//
slider29:1<0,16,1{All,Layer 1,Layer 2,Layer 3,Layer 4,Layer 5,Layer 6,Layer 7,Layer 8,Layer 9,Layer 10,Layer 11,Layer 12,Layer 13,Layer 14,Layer 15,Layer 16}>Layer
// Active/Inactive, per layer, and NOT the same thing as a level of -60.
// -60 is "this layer is silent because that is the level I want"; Inactive is
// "silence this layer and give it back to me later" -- the level you tuned is
// still there when you switch it on again. The suite convention (Vn Active in
// polyrhythm) and the reason it exists: a mute you can undo without having to
// remember a number. Both routes cost the same nothing at runtime, because the
// gate is the layer's gain and the whole layer's DSP is skipped when it is 0.
// Sits directly under the selector, ahead of the level: "is this layer in
// play" is the first question you ask about a layer, and it is a binary you
// can hear the answer to instantly.
slider30:1<0,1,1{Inactive,Active}>Layer active
// Defaults to 0, NOT -60, because the Layer selector defaults to the Original
// and this slider is stamped straight into whatever layer that selects. At -60
// it muted the Original on load -- on a fresh instance, and on every pre-layers
// project too, where REAPER supplies this slider's declared default because the
// saved line has no value for it. Two defaults that have to agree: the level
// slider's default is the default-selected layer's level.
slider35:0<-60,0,0.01>Layer level (dB, -60 = off)
slider36:0<0,1,1{Off,Solo}>Layer solo
slider31:0<-96,96,0.1>Layer pitch value (Hz / semitones / cents)
slider32:1<0,2,1{Hz,Semitones,Cents}>Layer pitch unit
slider33:0<-1000,1000,0.001>Layer fine tune
slider34:2<0,2,1{Hz,Semitones,Cents}>Layer fine tune unit
// CPU control, not a tone control. Every layer is its own set of oscillators --
// nothing about them can be shared with the main voice or with each other, so
// cost is layers x harmonics. A background layer rarely needs the same harmonic
// count as the lead voice to read as "there." 0 = uncapped (this layer gets
// whatever the source has, same as before this slider existed -- so it defaults
// to no change in sound for every project saved before this landed). A plain
// number, nudged by ear against CPU meter or crackle; no unit conversion, no
// percentage, the machine does the min() against what the source actually has.
// NOT a Drift or Ramp target, deliberately (2026-09-11): because it is a CPU cap, a
// drift on it would make the processing load rise and fall with the wave. Rozaya
// agreed, asking that the reason be written down.
slider37:0<0,64,1>Layer harmonics (0 = full)
// Per-layer overtone harmonic. The global Overtone harmonic above is folded into
// the shared magnitude array, so one setting applied to every layer at once, by
// harmonic INDEX -- with Overtone harmonic 8 the lift lands at 8*f0 in the
// Original, 4*f0 an octave down, 16*f0 an octave up. Consonant, because the
// layers are octaves and fifths, but not controllable: there was no way to say
// "overtone on the lead, none on the drone", which is what a throat-singing
// patch actually wants. The harmonic is the melodic choice -- it is a Drift and
// Ramp target, which is how the overtone melody gets played -- so it is the half
// that goes per layer. Lift and width stay global.
// -1 = follow the global, and is the default, so every layer behaves exactly as
// it did before this slider existed until you say otherwise.
slider38:-1<-1,64,1>Layer overtone harmonic (-1 = follow the global)

// --- Levels. Both are global and both belong at the end of the sound section,
//     after everything they scale. ---
slider39:0<-60,12,0.01>Input level (dry, dB)
// Named "Voice level" since this plugin was born, and wrong the whole time: it
// is applied to voutL/voutR, which is the equal-power crossfade of the voice
// AND the wash, after every layer has been summed into both. So it has always
// scaled the wash too, and now it scales all sixteen layers as well. The only
// thing it does not touch is the dry input, which has its own Input level.
// Relabelled rather than re-scoped -- the behaviour is right, the name was not.
slider40:0<-60,12,0.01>Output level (dB, everything but the dry input)

// --- TRANSPORT (canonical block B) -----------------------------------------
//     Start delay, then the Play/Rest gate. Added 2026-09-04: the Morpher had
//     neither, while every generator in the suite did and the plan lists it,
//     Passage and Veil as the three that were missing it.
//
//     ALL THREE TIMES FOLLOW `Rate Mode` ABOVE. Seconds, they are seconds; Beats,
//     they are beats, exactly as Auto-morph time already worked. That is why
//     this block adds no unit selector of its own -- the plugin already had one
//     switch for this and R11 says one sync block per plugin, not one per
//     control. Flipping the switch CONVERTS these values at the current tempo,
//     so nothing changes audibly at the moment you flip it; only the unit you
//     type in does.
//
//     Placed here rather than appended, because appending is a workaround for
//     not being able to migrate saved state and this suite migrates it (R18).
//     Everything from Drift target up moved +5; tools/morpher_migrate_transport.py
//     carries the projects across.
slider41:0<0,1000,0.001>Start delay (sec, or beats in Host x)

//     Play/Rest: run for a while, go quiet for a while, repeat. 0 in EITHER
//     one disables the gate, so a half-set pair never silently mutes anything.
slider42:0<0,1000,0.001>Play for (sec, or beats in Host x, 0 = always)
slider43:0<0,1000,0.001>Rest for (sec, or beats in Host x, 0 = always)

//     What the morph walk does while resting. Walk through keeps Auto-morph
//     advancing silently, so you rejoin wherever it has got to; Freeze in place
//     stops it and resumes exactly where it stopped.
//
//     CALLED "Rest mode", which is one of the suite's own two names for this
//     control -- Melody Phase, Shepard Scale and Shepard Tone all use it, and
//     the three filters call the same thing "LFO at rest". Took the name used by
//     the plugins whose moving part is not literally a filter LFO.
//
//     It was briefly "Modulation at rest", then briefly "Rest mode".
//     Both were invented for this one file. Rozaya, on the first: "since when
//     has anything in here ever been referenced as 'modulation'." Nowhere.
//
//     The invented name came with an invented SCOPE, which was the worse half.
//     The sweeping filters' "LFO at rest" freezes the sweep LFO and nothing
//     else -- Drift and Ramp run straight through a rest, everywhere in the
//     suite. This froze them too. It now governs the morph walk only, which is
//     this plugin's actual equivalent of an LFO: the thing that moves the sound
//     on a clock of its own. Drift has its own play/rest if you want it to hold.
slider44:0<0,1,1{Walk through,Freeze in place}>Rest mode

//     What comes OUT while resting. Pass-through leaves the dry input alone and
//     mutes only what this plugin adds -- the right default for an effect, and
//     it means a rest is a hole in the texture rather than a hole in the track.
//     Silence mutes everything.
slider45:0<0,1,1{Pass-through,Silence}>Output at rest

// --- Drift: suite-wide automation replacement (nested-selector pattern).
//     Pick a target; it wanders by up/down amounts over a wall-clock period.
//     All targets drift in parallel; the selector only chooses which one the
//     amount/period/shape sliders are editing. ---
// EIGHTY-SEVEN since 2026-09-13 (the pitch layout), in the order of the controls they reach,
// with an "all layers" entry for each layer control (R24, Polyrhythm's model). The plugin
// remaps an old save; tools/morpher_migrate_20260913.py remaps a project line's selectors.
slider46:0<0,86,1{Morph,Auto-morph time,Texture,Wash grain,Spread,Transpose,Fine tune,Tuning reference,Stereo width,Denoise,Low cut,High cut,Overtone harmonic,Overtone lift,Overtone width,Layer pitch (all layers),Layer 1 pitch,Layer 2 pitch,Layer 3 pitch,Layer 4 pitch,Layer 5 pitch,Layer 6 pitch,Layer 7 pitch,Layer 8 pitch,Layer 9 pitch,Layer 10 pitch,Layer 11 pitch,Layer 12 pitch,Layer 13 pitch,Layer 14 pitch,Layer 15 pitch,Layer 16 pitch,Layer fine tune (all layers),Layer 1 fine tune,Layer 2 fine tune,Layer 3 fine tune,Layer 4 fine tune,Layer 5 fine tune,Layer 6 fine tune,Layer 7 fine tune,Layer 8 fine tune,Layer 9 fine tune,Layer 10 fine tune,Layer 11 fine tune,Layer 12 fine tune,Layer 13 fine tune,Layer 14 fine tune,Layer 15 fine tune,Layer 16 fine tune,Layer level (all layers),Layer 1 level,Layer 2 level,Layer 3 level,Layer 4 level,Layer 5 level,Layer 6 level,Layer 7 level,Layer 8 level,Layer 9 level,Layer 10 level,Layer 11 level,Layer 12 level,Layer 13 level,Layer 14 level,Layer 15 level,Layer 16 level,Layer overtone harmonic (all layers),Layer 1 overtone harmonic,Layer 2 overtone harmonic,Layer 3 overtone harmonic,Layer 4 overtone harmonic,Layer 5 overtone harmonic,Layer 6 overtone harmonic,Layer 7 overtone harmonic,Layer 8 overtone harmonic,Layer 9 overtone harmonic,Layer 10 overtone harmonic,Layer 11 overtone harmonic,Layer 12 overtone harmonic,Layer 13 overtone harmonic,Layer 14 overtone harmonic,Layer 15 overtone harmonic,Layer 16 overtone harmonic,Input level,Output level,Play for,Rest for}>Drift target
slider47:0<0,1000,0.01>Drift up amount (per target, units match target)
slider48:0<0,1000,0.01>Drift down amount (per target, units match target)
slider49:0<0,12,1{Target default,Hz,Semitones,Cents,Milliseconds,Seconds,Minutes,BPM,Beats,Cycles,dB,Percent,Degrees}>Drift amount unit (per target, Target default where it cannot fit)
slider50:30<0,1000,0.01>Drift period (per target, in drift period units, 0 = off)
// Drift period unit. Same three options as everywhere else in the suite, but it
// declares SECONDS rather than the usual Cycles, for two reasons that agree:
// every one of the 122 instances in the library means seconds today (a declared
// default is a live value, so Cycles at index 0 would silently reinterpret all
// of them), and this is the only plugin whose cycle is OPTIONAL -- Cycles counts
// one Auto-morph traversal, and Auto-morph defaults to Off, which also hides its
// own rate control. A fresh instance defaulting to Cycles would have a drift
// that never advanced the first time it was switched on.
slider51:1<0,2,1{Cycles,Seconds,Beats}>Drift period unit (all targets)
slider52:0<0,2,1{Sine,Triangle,Random}>Drift shape (per target)
// --- Drift play/rest. Ported from Veil, the reference implementation, which
//     has been heard. Makes the wander move in bursts with holds between
//     instead of running evenly forever. PER TARGET, like the amounts it gates.
//     0 in either one = off, i.e. the old always-moving behaviour.
//
//     Windows are whole drift PERIODS, and the FRACTION chooses where the
//     parameter parks: x.25 freezes at the crest (drifted fully up), x.75 at
//     the trough, x.0 and x.5 at neutral. So an awkward fraction rotates the
//     park point, and a whole number parks at no-change EVERY time, which is
//     nearly inaudible. Confirmed by simulating the loop, not by reading it.
slider53:0<0,1000,0.01>Drift play for (per target, periods, 0 = always)
slider54:0<0,1000,0.01>Drift rest for (per target, periods, 0 = always)

// Restart on play = drift jumps to the start of its cycle when the transport
// begins (so multiple tracks reset together and stay in sync). Free-running =
// drift ignores the transport and evolves continuously (loop under/over an
// unbroken drift). A loop repeat is never a restart in either mode.
slider55:0<0,1,1{Restart on play,Free-running}>Drift restart (all targets)

// --- Ramp: the suite's "Speed Ramp" under an honest name (this plugin has no
//     speed to ramp). A one-time slow RIDE of a parameter: pick a target, how
//     far to move it (signed) and over how long, arm Engage, and it glides there
//     once and holds. Nested-selector like Drift; all targets ride in parallel
//     off one Engage; composes with Drift (base + drift wander + ramp ride). ---
slider56:0<0,86,1{Morph,Auto-morph time,Texture,Wash grain,Spread,Transpose,Fine tune,Tuning reference,Stereo width,Denoise,Low cut,High cut,Overtone harmonic,Overtone lift,Overtone width,Layer pitch (all layers),Layer 1 pitch,Layer 2 pitch,Layer 3 pitch,Layer 4 pitch,Layer 5 pitch,Layer 6 pitch,Layer 7 pitch,Layer 8 pitch,Layer 9 pitch,Layer 10 pitch,Layer 11 pitch,Layer 12 pitch,Layer 13 pitch,Layer 14 pitch,Layer 15 pitch,Layer 16 pitch,Layer fine tune (all layers),Layer 1 fine tune,Layer 2 fine tune,Layer 3 fine tune,Layer 4 fine tune,Layer 5 fine tune,Layer 6 fine tune,Layer 7 fine tune,Layer 8 fine tune,Layer 9 fine tune,Layer 10 fine tune,Layer 11 fine tune,Layer 12 fine tune,Layer 13 fine tune,Layer 14 fine tune,Layer 15 fine tune,Layer 16 fine tune,Layer level (all layers),Layer 1 level,Layer 2 level,Layer 3 level,Layer 4 level,Layer 5 level,Layer 6 level,Layer 7 level,Layer 8 level,Layer 9 level,Layer 10 level,Layer 11 level,Layer 12 level,Layer 13 level,Layer 14 level,Layer 15 level,Layer 16 level,Layer overtone harmonic (all layers),Layer 1 overtone harmonic,Layer 2 overtone harmonic,Layer 3 overtone harmonic,Layer 4 overtone harmonic,Layer 5 overtone harmonic,Layer 6 overtone harmonic,Layer 7 overtone harmonic,Layer 8 overtone harmonic,Layer 9 overtone harmonic,Layer 10 overtone harmonic,Layer 11 overtone harmonic,Layer 12 overtone harmonic,Layer 13 overtone harmonic,Layer 14 overtone harmonic,Layer 15 overtone harmonic,Layer 16 overtone harmonic,Input level,Output level,Play for,Rest for}>Ramp target
slider57:0<-1000,1000,0.001>Ramp by (per target, units match target)
slider58:0<0,12,1{Target default,Hz,Semitones,Cents,Milliseconds,Seconds,Minutes,BPM,Beats,Cycles,dB,Percent,Degrees}>Ramp by unit (per target, Target default where it cannot fit)
// Ramp time unit -- the unit for Ramp duration, Ramp play/rest and Ramp start
// delay, all four together. Default MINUTES (index 2), which is the suite
// default AND exactly what these four already meant, so no saved instance
// changes meaning.
//
// This RESOLVES the note that stood here: the ramp times were left "not
// sync-aware, deliberately", because tying them to Rate Mode meant a flip would
// have to CONVERT the number into beats, and the range could not hold the
// answer -- a 20-minute ramp at 120 BPM is 2400 beats -- so a long ramp would
// have been silently clamped. An explicit unit control does not convert
// anything: the number stays put and its unit is what you chose, which is how
// every other plugin in the suite does it. Beats are reachable now, and a beat
// count too large for the range is one you can see rather than one applied
// behind you.
slider59:2<0,3,1{Cycles,Seconds,Minutes,Beats}>Ramp time unit (all targets)
// 0..1000, widened 2026-09-06. `half-music-half-wind.RPP` has 120 typed into
// this on two instances -- a two-hour ramp, above the one hour the control
// used to offer.
slider60:0<0,1000,0.1>Ramp duration (per target, in ramp time units)
// Ramp play/rest turns the smooth ride into a STAIRCASE -- "ramp on one side,
// stairs on the other" (Rozaya, 2026-09-04). Windows are whole RAMP TIME
// UNITS, matching Ramp duration. The rests come OUT of the duration
// rather than extending it:
// while moving it moves faster, by exactly (play+rest)/play, so a stepped ride
// and a smooth one of the same length make the same journey in the same time --
// crossing each other on the way, and landing on the destination together.
slider61:0<0,1000,0.01>Ramp play for (per target, 0 = smooth)
slider62:0<0,1000,0.01>Ramp rest for (per target, 0 = smooth)
slider63:0<0,1,1{Off,On}>Ramp engage (all targets)
slider64:0<0,1000,0.1>Ramp start delay (per target, in ramp time units)

@init
// ext_noinit = 1: @init runs ONLY on plugin load, so captured slots (and all
// memory) survive transport stop/play -- the whole point of a capture-based
// instrument. RUNTIME state (accumulators, positions, smoother) is reset
// explicitly on the transport play-edge in @block instead, so playback still
// restarts cleanly (and renders correctly) without wiping the captures.
ext_noinit = 1;
TWOPI = 6.283185307179586; PI = 3.141592653589793;
NSLOTS = 8; MAXFFT = 32768; INRING = 65536;
N_TARGETS = 87;  // Drift/Ramp targets, in CONTROL ORDER (2026-09-13, the pitch layout):
                 // 0 Morph, 1 Auto-morph time, 2 Texture, 3 Wash grain, 4 Spread, 5 Transpose,
                 // 6 Fine tune, 7 Tuning reference, 8 Stereo width, 9 Denoise, 10 Low cut,
                 // 11 High cut, 12 Overtone harmonic, 13 Overtone lift, 14 Overtone width,
                 // 15 Layer pitch (all layers), 16-31 Layer 1-16 pitch, 32 fine tune (all),
                 // 33-48 fine tunes, 49 level (all), 50-65 levels, 66 overtone harmonic (all),
                 // 67-82 overtone harmonics, 83 Input level, 84 Output level, 85 Play for,
                 // 86 Rest for. It was 55 (2026-09-11) and 24 before that; a save is remapped.
LP_T0  = 16;     // Layer k's pitch target (k = 0 is Layer 1)
LF_T0  = 33;     // Layer k's fine tune target
LEV_T0 = 50;     // Layer k's level target
LOT_T0 = 67;     // Layer k's overtone harmonic target
NLAYERS  = 15;   // pitch-shifted copies of the morph, all behind one selector
N_CUSTOM = 3;    // slots 0..12 are the fixed intervals, in pitch order:
                 // -48 -36 -24 -12 semitones, -7 -5, unison, +5 +7, +12 +24 +36 +48.
                 // The three free-interval Custom slots trail them at LAY_CUSTOM0.
                 // (This said 'layers 0..2 are the Custom layers' until 2026-09-01 --
                 // true of CANON, wrong since the list became a pitch ladder.)
LAY_T0   = 8;    // target index of layer 0 -- targets LAY_T0+i map to layer i
// The ORIGINAL -- the unshifted morph every layer stacks around -- gets a bank
// slot of its own at index NLAYERS, so Active, Level and Solo apply to it
// exactly as they do to a layer. It is not synthesised by the layer loops (it
// is the base path), so the synthesis loops still run 0..NLAYERS-1 while
// anything reading the CONFIG banks runs 0..N_LAY_SLOTS-1.
// The list is a PITCH LADDER: four octaves down up to four octaves up, with the
// original sitting at unison where it belongs -- between the fourth down and the
// fourth up. Putting it at either END of the list was the same problem twice: an
// entry out of musical order, just from opposite directions. The three Custom
// slots trail the ladder because an arbitrary interval has no place in it.
LAY_ORIG    = 0;            // Layer 1 since 2026-09-13 (it was 6, unison mid-ladder)
LAY_CUSTOM0 = 13;           // Custom 1..3 at slots 13,14,15 (lay_semi is indexed slot-LAY_CUSTOM0)
N_LAY_SLOTS = NLAYERS + 1;  // config-bank entries: the ladder, plus the three Custom slots
FFTSIZE = 32768; NBINS = 16385;
// WA = analysis window length, FIXED and decoupled from Wash grain. The capture
// analysis (heavy: FFT + YIN) happens at WA; Wash grain only rebuilds the cheap
// synthesis grain. That makes Wash grain safe to AUTOMATE (no re-FFT, no buffer
// clear -> no dropouts), and Capture point scrubs this WA-length window in the grab.
WA = 8192;   // ~170 ms @ 48k: good spectral resolution, leaves ~0.5 s scrub range
// Capture average: frames step by half a window, and CAPAVG_MAX is what fits
// inside one grab while leaving the capture point somewhere to move.
CAPAVG_HOP = WA / 2; CAPAVG_MAX = 6;
// Wash grain reaches its control's 1000 ms at ANY sample rate (2026-09-13). It stopped at
// MAXFFT samples -- 743 ms at 44.1 kHz, 680 at 48, 341 at 96 -- because a grain was one
// FFTSIZE inverse FFT, and JSFX's fft() stops at 32768 (REAPER's documentation; a round
// trip at 65536 measured silently wrong). A longer grain is now built from FFTSIZE pieces
// half-overlapping inside it, each with its own random phases; a grain that fits one piece
// is built exactly as before. GMAX samples is one second. Rozaya: "sounds good".
GMAX = ceil(srate);
// ACCLEN must exceed the LONGEST grain plus a hop, or a long grain wraps the circular
// accumulator onto the read pointer. 65536 still covers a second at 44.1 and 48 kHz.
ACCLEN = 65536; loop(8, ACCLEN < GMAX + GMAX/4 + 1 ? ACCLEN *= 2; );
NHARM = 64; WHARM = 3072;          // 64 harmonics; ~64 ms YIN analysis window
// Magnitudes are peak-normalised, so this is roughly -66 dB below the loudest
// partial: comfortably under anything audible under the rest of the series,
// and conservative enough that a bright capture keeps its whole tail.
HM_EPS = 0.0005;
WTBL = 8192; WTSCALE = WTBL / TWOPI;
// Voice stereo width: at full Stereo width the right-channel voice runs +-0.8%
// (~14 cents) detuned from the left, so L/R beat slowly -> width + de-robot
// shimmer. Tune by ear; bigger = wider/chorus-ier, smaller = tighter.
VOICE_DETUNE_MAX = 0.008;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Memory map: a RUNNING allocator, not hand-picked addresses. Every buffer is
// sized from the constants above, so NSLOTS (or any window size) can change
// without hand-editing offsets. The old fixed-address map collided the moment
// NSLOTS went past 4 (slotraw ran straight into slot_norm) -- this cannot.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
freemem = 0;
fftwork   = freemem; freemem += 2*FFTSIZE;    // FFT scratch (interleaved re/im)
inbuf     = freemem; freemem += INRING;        // live input ring
window    = freemem; freemem += WA;            // Hann of length WA (fixed analysis window)
synwin    = freemem; freemem += GMAX;          // sqrt-Hann synthesis grain (length W = Wash grain, up to 1 s)
accL      = freemem; freemem += ACCLEN;        // overlap-add accumulator L
accR      = freemem; freemem += ACCLEN;        // overlap-add accumulator R
mtmp      = freemem; freemem += NBINS;
curmag    = freemem; freemem += NBINS;
phase     = freemem; freemem += NBINS;
doff      = freemem; freemem += NBINS;         // per-bin stereo decorrelation offsets
grainbuf  = freemem; freemem += GMAX;          // a grain, left (and right, when a short grain reads it from fftwork)
grainR    = freemem; freemem += GMAX;          // a long grain's right channel, assembled piece by piece
// A long grain's piece window: periodic sqrt-Hann, so two pieces half a piece apart keep
// the power constant where they overlap (their squares sum to exactly 1). No rand().
pwin      = freemem; freemem += FFTSIZE;
pw_i = 0; loop(FFTSIZE, pwin[pw_i] = sqrt(0.5 - 0.5*cos(6.283185307179586*pw_i/FFTSIZE)); pw_i += 1; );
// --- per-slot capture stores (ALL sized by NSLOTS -- this is what makes the
//     slot count a free dial) ---
slotmag   = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS*NBINS;  // wash (spectral) magnitudes
slotraw   = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS*MAXFFT; // raw captured audio (source of truth)
slot_norm = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;        // wash peak normalization
slot_harm = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS*NHARM;  // voice harmonic magnitudes
slot_f0   = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;        // detected fundamentals (Hz)
slot_hnorm= freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;        // voice peak normalization
slot_capavg   = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;    // Capture average (frames) per slot. The CONTROL is global -- every entry holds the same value -- but the storage stays per slot so compute_spectrum reads it the same way and the @serialize field keeps its byte format
ot_gain   = freemem; freemem += NHARM;         // per-harmonic Overtone gain (1 = untouched)
hm_rawA   = freemem; freemem += NHARM;         // partial magnitudes BEFORE any Overtone, voice A
hm_rawB   = freemem; freemem += NHARM;         // ...and voice B. The divisions live here once.
hmA_arr   = freemem; freemem += NHARM;         // per-sample partial magnitudes, voice A
hmB_arr   = freemem; freemem += NHARM;         // ...and voice B. Hoisted out of the layer loops.
slot_cappoint = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;    // PER-SLOT capture point (0..1): where in each slot's grab to analyze. Each slot remembers its own scrub position.
// --- harmonic (voice) engine scratch ---
hph       = freemem; freemem += NHARM;         // resynth phase accumulators (voice A, left)
hphB      = freemem; freemem += NHARM;         // resynth phase accumulators (voice B, left)
hphR      = freemem; freemem += NHARM;         // voice A, right channel (detuned for width)
hphBR     = freemem; freemem += NHARM;         // voice B, right channel (detuned for width)
// Layers get their own phase accumulators -- one full set per layer, per morph
// bank (A/B), per channel. They cannot share the base banks: a layer runs at a
// different frequency, so a shared accumulator would be at the wrong phase for
// both. Sized off NLAYERS/NHARM like everything else here.
lay_hph   = freemem; freemem += N_LAY_SLOTS*NHARM;  // layer voice A, left
lay_hphB  = freemem; freemem += N_LAY_SLOTS*NHARM;  // layer voice B, left
lay_hphR  = freemem; freemem += N_LAY_SLOTS*NHARM;  // layer voice A, right
lay_hphBR = freemem; freemem += N_LAY_SLOTS*NHARM;  // layer voice B, right
// Layer config, derived in @slider (and per-sample when a level is drifting).
layer_ratio = freemem; freemem += 32;   // frequency multiplier from the interval
layer_gain  = freemem; freemem += 32;   // linear gain from the level (0 = off)
lay_semi    = freemem; freemem += 32;   // interval in semitones (the raw slider)
lay_db_base = freemem; freemem += 32;   // level in dB, before Drift/Ramp
lay_active  = freemem; freemem += 32;   // 1 = audible, 0 = muted but remembered
lay_solo    = freemem; freemem += 32;   // 1 = audition this layer alone
lay_nharm   = freemem; freemem += 32;   // per-layer harmonic cap, raw slider (0 = uncapped)
lay_ot_harm = freemem; freemem += 32;   // per-layer Overtone harmonic (-1 = follow the global)
// Per-layer Overtone working space. Only layers that actually override the
// global harmonic use their slice; the rest read the shared arrays, so a
// project that never touches this pays nothing for it beyond the allocation.
lay_ot_gain = freemem; freemem += N_LAY_SLOTS*NHARM;   // that layer's per-harmonic gain
lay_ot_norm = freemem; freemem += 32;   // that layer's power normalisation (wash reads it)
lot_bin     = freemem; freemem += 32;   // wash: that layer's overtone centre, in SOURCE bins
lay_hmA     = freemem; freemem += N_LAY_SLOTS*NHARM;   // ...and its partial magnitudes, bank A
lay_hmB     = freemem; freemem += N_LAY_SLOTS*NHARM;   //                                bank B
ser_scratch = freemem; freemem += 32;   // read-and-discard space for old @serialize layouts
lay_perm    = freemem; freemem += 32;   // CANON layer order -> the pitch ladder
lay_inv     = freemem; freemem += 32;   // ...and back, for the remembered selector positions
lay_unrot   = freemem; freemem += 32;   // 7700007 (original first) -> CANON
lpr         = freemem; freemem += 32;   // pitch_ratio * layer_ratio, per grain
anbuf     = freemem; freemem += WHARM;         // mono analysis frame
yin_df    = freemem; freemem += WHARM;         // YIN difference function
yin_cmnd  = freemem; freemem += WHARM;         // YIN cumulative-mean-normalized difference
// --- sine wavetable (render-speed optimization): the voice engine's per-sample
// sin() calls become a table lookup. Internal only -- no sliders, no pitch or
// behavior change (frequency is set by the phase INCREMENT, which is untouched). ---
sintab    = freemem; freemem += WTBL+2;        // one sine period + a guard sample for interp
driftbag  = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;        // Auto-morph Shuffle bag (even random slot coverage)
// --- suite Drift: per-target config + runtime banks. Re-spaced 16 -> 32 when
//     the layer levels took the target count past 16, exactly as melody_phase
//     did at 28 targets: at 16 the banks are contiguous, so target 16 of one
//     bank IS target 0 of the next. Spacing is allocation only -- @serialize
//     writes n_ser_targets from each bank's own base, so the format is unmoved. ---
// Re-spaced 32 -> 64 on 2026-09-11, when the target list reached 55, and 64 -> 128 on 2026-09-13
// when it reached 87.
target_drift_up     = freemem; freemem += 128;  // CONFIG: up amount   (survives transport)
target_drift_down   = freemem; freemem += 128;  // CONFIG: down amount
target_drift_per    = freemem; freemem += 128;  // CONFIG: period (seconds)
target_drift_shape  = freemem; freemem += 128;  // CONFIG: 0 Sine / 1 Triangle / 2 Random
target_drift_phase  = freemem; freemem += 128;  // RUNTIME: 0..1 phase (reset on play)
target_drift_prev   = freemem; freemem += 128;  // RUNTIME: Random shape prev value
target_drift_curr   = freemem; freemem += 128;  // RUNTIME: Random shape target value
target_drift_offset = freemem; freemem += 128;  // RUNTIME: current offset (read at consumption)
target_drift_play   = freemem; freemem += 128;  // CONFIG: play window, drift periods (0 = always)
target_drift_rest   = freemem; freemem += 128;  // CONFIG: rest window, drift periods
drift_pr_resting    = freemem; freemem += 128;  // RUNTIME: 1 = frozen
drift_pr_accum      = freemem; freemem += 128;  // RUNTIME: fractional periods into this window
ramp_play_mem       = freemem; freemem += 128;  // CONFIG: play window, ramp minutes (0 = smooth)
ramp_rest_mem       = freemem; freemem += 128;  // CONFIG: rest window, ramp minutes
ramp_pr_resting     = freemem; freemem += 128;  // RUNTIME: 1 = holding
ramp_pr_accum       = freemem; freemem += 128;  // RUNTIME: fractional minutes into this window
td_active           = freemem; freemem += 128;  // @slider: 1 if a target has any drift configured
// --- Ramp: per-target config + runtime banks (64-slot-spaced, as above) ---
ramp_by_mem            = freemem; freemem += 128;  // CONFIG: signed `by` amount
ramp_dur_mem           = freemem; freemem += 128;  // CONFIG: duration (minutes)
ramp_delay_mem         = freemem; freemem += 128;  // CONFIG: start delay (minutes)
ramp_t_mem             = freemem; freemem += 128;  // RUNTIME: 0..1 ride progress (reset on play)
ramp_delay_elapsed_mem = freemem; freemem += 128;  // RUNTIME: start-delay sample counter
ramp_offset_mem        = freemem; freemem += 128;  // RUNTIME: ramp_t * by (read at consumption)
mod_active             = freemem; freemem += 128;  // @slider: 1 if a target has drift OR ramp configured
mo_order               = freemem; freemem += 128;  // the drift loop's order: the old twenty-four first
dui_last               = freemem; freemem += 16;  // mirror of the eleven visible Drift/Ramp controls
lay_ot_eff             = freemem; freemem += 32;  // each layer's overtone harmonic with drift and ramp
lay_punit   = freemem; freemem += 32;   // each layer's pitch unit {Hz, Semitones, Cents} (2026-09-13)
lay_fine    = freemem; freemem += 32;   // each layer's fine tune
lay_funit   = freemem; freemem += 32;   // ...and its unit
lay_lad     = freemem; freemem += 32;   // Layer k (0 = Layer 1) -> its index in the old pitch ladder
lay_ladinv  = freemem; freemem += 32;   // ...and back
lay_ui_last = freemem; freemem += 16;   // mirror of the nine visible layer controls
tg_scratch  = freemem; freemem += 128;  // @init's and @serialize's copy of a 55-target list
// Drift amount unit and Ramp by unit, per target (2026-09-13). 0 = Target default.
target_drift_unit = freemem; freemem += 128;
ramp_by_unit      = freemem; freemem += 128;
// The thirteen ladder layers' intervals, as Layers 1-13 start. Layer 1, the Original, is 0.
function lay_seed_ladder() (
  lay_semi[0] = 0;
  lay_semi[1] = -48; lay_semi[2] = -36; lay_semi[3] = -24; lay_semi[4] = -12; lay_semi[5] = -7; lay_semi[6] = -5;
  lay_semi[7] = 5;   lay_semi[8] = 7;   lay_semi[9] = 12;  lay_semi[10] = 24; lay_semi[11] = 36; lay_semi[12] = 48;
);
i = 0; loop(WTBL+1, sintab[i] = sin(TWOPI*i/WTBL); i += 1; );
// captured slots persist across transport play (one-time init)
slots_inited == 0 ? (
  slots_inited = 1;
  i = 0; loop(NSLOTS, slot_norm[i] = 1; slot_cappoint[i] = 0; slot_capavg[i] = 1; i += 1; );  // per-slot capture point defaults to 0 (earliest); @serialize restores/migrates
  i = 0; loop(NBINS, doff[i] = rand(1)*2 - 1; i += 1; );
  // have / n_used live in this ONE-TIME guard so captures survive transport
  // play (@init re-runs on every play; if these were reset there, a capture
  // made in one play would be orphaned on the next).
  have = 0; n_used = 0;
  // Drift CONFIG defaults live in the same one-time guard, for the same reason:
  // they must survive transport play. @serialize overwrites these with the
  // saved config on project load (the guard runs on fresh load, BEFORE the
  // @serialize restore — the exact order the captures above already rely on).
  i = 0; loop(N_TARGETS,
    target_drift_up[i]    = 0;
    target_drift_down[i]  = 0;
    target_drift_per[i]   = 30;
    target_drift_shape[i] = 0;
    target_drift_play[i]  = 0;
    target_drift_rest[i]  = 0;
    ramp_play_mem[i]      = 0;
    ramp_rest_mem[i]      = 0;
    target_drift_unit[i]  = 0;   // Target default: today's meaning
    ramp_by_unit[i]       = 0;
    ramp_by_mem[i]    = 0;   // Ramp CONFIG defaults (same one-time-guard rationale)
    ramp_dur_mem[i]   = 0;   // 0 duration = ramp inert until the user sets it
    ramp_delay_mem[i] = 0;
    i += 1;
  );
  last_target_select = 0;
  last_ramp_target   = 0;
  // Layer bank defaults. Every layer starts OFF, so the whole feature is inert
  // until a level is raised -- and one level IS the whole gesture, because the
  // interval is already decided by which layer you picked. These live in the
  // one-time guard for the same reason the captures do: they must survive the
  // transport, and @serialize overwrites them with the saved bank on load.
  lpi = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, lay_db_base[lpi] = -60; lay_active[lpi] = 1; layer_gain[lpi] = 0; layer_ratio[lpi] = 1; lpi += 1; );
  // The original defaults to UNITY, not off -- it is the sound, and a default
  // of -60 here would open every existing project silent.
  lay_db_base[LAY_ORIG] = 0; layer_gain[LAY_ORIG] = 1;
  // The three Custom layers start somewhere musical (an octave down, an octave
  // up, two octaves down) so that raising one is immediately worth hearing.
  // Every layer has a pitch (2026-09-13): the ladder it replaces, then Custom 1-3's old
  // defaults, in Semitones with no fine tune. A fresh instance is the instrument it was.
  lay_seed_ladder();
  lay_semi[13] = -12; lay_semi[14] = 12; lay_semi[15] = -24;
  lpi = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, lay_punit[lpi] = 1; lay_fine[lpi] = 0; lay_funit[lpi] = 2; lpi += 1; );
  lpi = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, lay_solo[lpi] = 0; lay_nharm[lpi] = 0; lay_ot_harm[lpi] = -1; lpi += 1; );
  // Unity, not zero: the wash multiplies by this, and `ot_wash` gating every
  // read is a guard, not a reason to leave a gain bank at silence.
  lpi = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, lay_ot_norm[lpi] = 1; lot_bin[lpi] = 0; lpi += 1; );
  last_lay_sel = LAY_ORIG;
  last_cap_slot      = 0;   // per-slot Capture-point selector (mirrors the Drift/Ramp target selectors)
  last_cap_sel = 1; last_cap_all = 0; cp_ui_last = 0; cap_all_scrub = 0;   // All slots (0 = All, 1-8 = Slot 1-8)
);
// Drift RUNTIME state (phase/prev/curr/offset) is reset here on load AND on the
// transport play-edge in @block, so drift restarts at phase 0 on every play
// (continues across stop) — matching the rest of the suite.
i = 0; loop(N_TARGETS,
  target_drift_phase[i] = 0; target_drift_prev[i] = 0;
  target_drift_curr[i]  = 0; target_drift_offset[i] = 0;
  drift_pr_resting[i]   = 0; drift_pr_accum[i]      = 0;
  ramp_pr_resting[i]    = 0; ramp_pr_accum[i]       = 0;
  ramp_t_mem[i] = 0; ramp_delay_elapsed_mem[i] = 0; ramp_offset_mem[i] = 0;
  i += 1;
);
eff_spread_hz = 0; eff_lowcut_hz = 0; eff_stereo_width = 0;   // seeded properly in @slider/@sample
// runtime state
cap_last = 0; first_slider = 1; inpos = 0; last_gsamp = -1; last_cappoint = 0;
cap_dirty = 0; capavg_dirty = 0; settle = 0; last_capavg = 1;   // debounce for Capture-point / Capture-average re-analysis (capavg_dirty = the re-derive covers ALL slots, not just the focused one)
// Captured spectra re-derive LAZILY -- only when actually stale (a fresh project
// load, or a capture-point scrub), never on an ordinary stop/play. In the normal
// capture-then-play flow nothing re-derives at all. Starts stale on load
// (@serialize restores the raw audio but not the analysis derived from it).
analysis_valid = 0;
rms_smooth = 0;   // slow auto-gain state (stable loudness, no per-grain pump)
cap_point01 = 0;  // analysis position in the grab (slider3); 0 = earliest
texture = 0.5; hlevel = 0.707; wlevel = 0.707;   // voice<->wash crossfade
i = 0; loop(NHARM, hph[i] = rand(1)*TWOPI; hphB[i] = rand(1)*TWOPI; hphR[i] = hph[i]; hphBR[i] = hphB[i]; i += 1; );
// Layer phases: random start per layer, R seeded from L (they part company only
// once Stereo width detunes them), mirroring the base banks above.
// Filled in the OLD ladder's memory order (2026-09-13): Layer 1 became the Original, so the
// six layers below it moved one slot along. Each layer keeps the starting phases it always
// had, so a saved stack starts exactly as it did. Ladder slot s is Layer 1 at s = 6, Layer
// s + 2 below it, and keeps its slot above it.
i = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS*NHARM,
  lpo_s = floor(i / NHARM);
  lpo_k = (lpo_s == 6 ? 0 : lpo_s < 6 ? lpo_s + 1 : lpo_s) * NHARM + (i - lpo_s * NHARM);
  lay_hph[lpo_k] = rand(1)*TWOPI; lay_hphB[lpo_k] = rand(1)*TWOPI;
  lay_hphR[lpo_k] = lay_hph[lpo_k];   lay_hphBR[lpo_k] = lay_hphB[lpo_k];
  i += 1;
);
// The ladder's fixed ratios became every layer's own pitch on 2026-09-13; @block derives
// layer_ratio for all sixteen. lay_lad maps Layer k to where it sat in the ladder, which is
// how an older save's layer banks, and (until the 87-target list) the layer targets, are found.
lay_lad[0] = 6;
lpi = 1; loop(6, lay_lad[lpi] = lpi - 1; lpi += 1; );
lpi = 7; loop(9, lay_lad[lpi] = lpi; lpi += 1; );
lpi = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, lay_ladinv[lay_lad[lpi]] = lpi; lpi += 1; );
// --- Layer-order migration tables.
// CANON is the order every layout up to 7700006 used: Custom 1..3, then the
// twelve intervals low to high, then the original last. lay_perm maps a slot in
// the NEW pitch ladder to where that layer sat in CANON, so new[k] =
// canon[lay_perm[k]]. lay_inv is the reverse, for remembered selector indices.
lay_perm[0]  = 3;  lay_perm[1]  = 4;  lay_perm[2]  = 5;  lay_perm[3]  = 6;
lay_perm[4]  = 7;  lay_perm[5]  = 8;  lay_perm[6]  = 15; lay_perm[7]  = 9;
lay_perm[8]  = 10; lay_perm[9]  = 11; lay_perm[10] = 12; lay_perm[11] = 13;
lay_perm[12] = 14; lay_perm[13] = 0;  lay_perm[14] = 1;  lay_perm[15] = 2;
lpi = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, lay_inv[lay_perm[lpi]] = lpi; lpi += 1; );
// 7700007 briefly put the original FIRST. Undo that rotate to reach CANON, then
// the table above finishes the job -- two small steps instead of a second table.
lpi = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, lay_unrot[lpi] = lpi >= N_LAY_SLOTS-1 ? 0 : lpi + 1; lpi += 1; );
// High cut / Layers runtime defaults: OFF, and off means the arithmetic below
// multiplies by exactly 1.0 -- bit-identical to the engine before they existed.
hicut_hz = 20000; eff_hicut_hz = 20000; hc_on = 0;
hc_top = srate*0.5; hc_lo = 1000000000; hc_inv = 0; hc_bound = srate*0.5;   // EEL2 has NO scientific notation -- 1e9 is a syntax error
hcbin_hi = NBINS + 1; hcbin_lo = NBINS + 1; hcbin_inv = 0;
nlay_on = 0; any_solo = 0; base_gain = 1; nharm_used = NHARM;
// Overtone: flat gains (off), smoothers settled, ~30 ms to take the step out
// of a manual move -- each control is a gain change across dozens of partials.
i = 0; loop(NHARM, ot_gain[i] = 1; i += 1; );
ot_coeff = 1 - exp(-1 / (0.03 * srate));
ot_harm_base = 0; ot_depth_db = 24; ot_width = 1;
ot_h_smooth = 0; ot_db_smooth = 24; ot_w_smooth = 1;
ot_on_last = 0; ot_h_last = -1; ot_db_last = -1; ot_w_last = -1;
ot_boost = 1; ot_norm = 1; ot_bin = 0; ot_binw = 1; ot_f0bin = 0;
voice_detune = 0;   // right-channel voice detune amount (from Stereo width)
i = 0; loop(WA, window[i] = 0.5 - 0.5*cos(TWOPI*i/(WA-1)); i += 1; );   // analysis window (fixed)
morph_phase = 0; eff_morph01 = 0; last_automode = -1; last_ps = 0;
drift_was_playing = 0;   // Drift restart-on-play edge state
cur_slot = 0; nxt_slot = 0; xfade = 0;             // Drift: direct crossfade cur_slot -> nxt_slot
// Previous sample's morph pair, for the voice phase handover in @sample. -1 so
// the first sample records the pair without copying anything.
last_vsi = -1; last_vsj = -1;
bag_pos = 999; last_slot = 0; drift_primed = 0;    // Drift shuffle-bag state (primed lazily in @sample)

// --- Transport runtime (Start delay + Play/Rest), added 2026-09-04 ---------
// RUNTIME, not config, so it belongs OUTSIDE the drift_cfg_inited guard and is
// deliberately re-zeroed on every transport play. That is the suite's Path A
// convention: a fresh play starts the delay again and re-arms the gate, which
// is what "sit silent for N seconds after I press play" has to mean.
start_delay_elapsed = 0;
pr_resting          = 0;
pr_accum            = 0;
// Derived in @block from the sliders; seeded here so the first @sample before
// the first @block reads something sane rather than an implicit zero.
start_delay_sec = 0; pr_play_sec = 0; pr_rest_sec = 0; pr_enabled = 0;
morph_at_rest = 0; out_at_rest = 0; tr_k = 1; drift_per_k = 1;
// Seconds-per-unit factors, seeded to the DECLARED defaults of their unit
// controls (Drift period unit = Seconds, Ramp time unit = Minutes) so the
// very first block cannot read a zero and stall a period. @block overwrites
// both every block thereafter.
drift_unit_sec = 1; ramp_unit_sec = 60;
in_rest = 0; in_start_delay = 0; mod_frozen = 0; morph_frozen = 0;
// Scramble the RNG per load: EEL rand() otherwise replays the SAME sequence
// every session, so Drift would wander the identical path each time you open the
// project. Advance it a sub-second-time-dependent number of steps so each load
// starts somewhere different. One-time, bounded, ~free.
_tp = time_precise(); i = 0; loop(floor(1 + (_tp - floor(_tp)) * 500), rand(1); i += 1; );

// --- The eighty-seven targets (2026-09-13) ------------------------------------
// The four "all layers" entries -- 15 pitch, 32 fine tune, 49 level, 66 overtone harmonic --
// hold nothing of their own, on Polyrhythm's model: reading one shows Layer 1's, writing it
// reaches all sixteen.
function mo_is_all(t) ( t == 15 || t == 32 || t == 49 || t == 66; );
function mo_read_slot(t) ( mo_is_all(t) ? t + 1 : t; );
function mo_write(t, tbank, tval) local(mw_k) (
  mo_is_all(t) ? (
    mw_k = 1; loop(16, tbank[t + mw_k] = tval; mw_k += 1;);
  ) : (
    tbank[t] = tval;
  );
);
// Change-detected: parked on an "all" entry, a wholesale capture would flatten
// sixteen layers' setups on any stray @slider pass.
function mo_capture(t, tbank, tval, tslot) (
  tval != dui_last[tslot] ? ( mo_write(t, tbank, tval); dui_last[tslot] = tval; );
);
// A 24-target save's index, in this list.
function mo_o2n(o) (
  o == 0 ? 2 : o == 1 ? 4 : o == 2 ? 5 : o == 3 ? 6 : o == 4 ? 8 : o == 5 ? 52 :
  o == 6 ? 10 : o == 7 ? 9 : o + 6;
);
// A 55-target save's index (2026-09-11), in this list. Its layer entries were in the old
// pitch ladder's order, so lay_ladinv finds each layer. Its "Layer pitch (all Custom layers)"
// held nothing and no saved copy was parked on it (135 read); it becomes "all layers".
function t55_o2n(o) (
  o <= 5 ? o : o <= 12 ? o + 2 : o == 13 ? 49 : o <= 29 ? LEV_T0 + lay_ladinv[o - 14] :
  o == 30 ? 15 : o <= 33 ? LP_T0 + 13 + (o - 31) : o == 34 ? 66 : o <= 50 ? LOT_T0 + lay_ladinv[o - 35] :
  o + 32;
);
function t87_remap(bank, dflt) local(tr_k) (
  tr_k = 0; loop(55, tg_scratch[tr_k] = bank[tr_k]; tr_k += 1;);
  tr_k = 0; loop(N_TARGETS, bank[tr_k] = dflt; tr_k += 1;);
  tr_k = 0; loop(55, bank[t55_o2n(tr_k)] = tg_scratch[tr_k]; tr_k += 1;);
);
function mo_remap(bank, dflt) local(mr_k) (
  mr_k = 0; loop(24, ser_scratch[mr_k] = bank[mr_k]; mr_k += 1;);
  mr_k = 0; loop(N_TARGETS, bank[mr_k] = dflt; mr_k += 1;);
  mr_k = 0; loop(24, bank[mo_o2n(mr_k)] = ser_scratch[mr_k]; mr_k += 1;);
);
// Drift + ramp on one target, in its own unit.
function mo_q(k) ( target_drift_offset[k] + ramp_offset_mem[k]; );
// --- The pitch block (2026-09-13), Passage's functions. Bubbler's model: every pitch value
// resolves to SEMITONES. Hz counts from the Tuning reference; a Source fine tune in Hz counts
// from the source note's own frequency. tuning_ref is the heard reference: its base plus any
// Drift or Ramp.
tuning_ref = 440; tuning_base = 440;
function pv_semis(u, v) local(r) (
  r = max(tuning_ref, 0.001);
  u == 0 ? 12 * log(max(r + v, 0.001) / r) / log(2) : u == 2 ? v / 100 : v;
);
function pv_src_semis(idx, u, v) local(f) (
  f = max(tuning_ref, 0.001) * pow(2, (idx - 1 - 69) / 12);
  u == 0 ? 12 * log(max(f + v, 0.001) / f) / log(2) : u == 2 ? v / 100 : v;
);
pv_inited = 0;   // the Source/Target note trackers are adopted in @block
// --- The amount units (2026-09-13), Passage's. A Drift or Ramp amount is held in the unit its
// picker names, {0 Target default, 1 Hz, 2 Semitones, 3 Cents, 4 Milliseconds, 5 Seconds,
// 6 Minutes, 7 BPM, 8 Beats, 9 Cycles, 10 dB, 11 Percent, 12 Degrees}, and turned into the
// target's OWN unit here, against the value it rides on. A unit that cannot fit acts as Target
// default -- Rozaya: "It should fall back to the target's native unit, if one's not already
// been set :)". Percent, dB and count targets therefore take every amount as it stands.
//
// A pitch value (Transpose, Fine tune, a layer's pitch and fine tune) in nu {0 Hz, 1 Semitones,
// 2 Cents}: Hz counts from the Tuning reference, and an Hz amount moves the heard frequency.
function au_pitch(off, u, base, nu) local(s, s2, r) (
  (u < 1 || u > 3) || (u == 1 && nu == 0) || (u == 2 && nu == 1) || (u == 3 && nu == 2) ? off : (
    r = max(tuning_ref, 0.001);
    s = pv_semis(nu, base);
    s2 = u == 2 ? s + off : u == 3 ? s + off / 100 : 12 * log(max(r * pow(2, s / 12) + off, 0.001) / r) / log(2);
    (nu == 1 ? s2 : nu == 2 ? s2 * 100 : r * pow(2, s2 / 12) - r) - base;
  );
);
// A frequency in Hz (Spread, Low cut, High cut, Tuning reference): Semitones and Cents move it
// by an interval, counted from the value, or from `floor` below it -- a cutoff at 0 still moves
// (Rozaya: "I'd expect it to do as advertised lol"). Floors, Passage's: 20 Hz for the cutoffs
// and the Tuning reference, one FFT bin for Spread.
function au_freq(off, u, base, floor) local(b) (
  b = max(base, floor);
  u == 2 ? b * pow(2, off / 12) - base : u == 3 ? b * pow(2, off / 1200) - base : off;
);
// A length (Wash grain, Play for, Rest for) in nu {0 Seconds, 1 Hz, 2 Beats, 3 Milliseconds}.
// Time units add time; Hz and BPM move the rate the length is one cycle of. A Cycle is one
// Auto-morph traversal, as in the period units (au_cyc, set per block).
au_cyc = 20;
function au_sec(nu, v) ( nu == 3 ? v / 1000 : nu == 1 ? (v > 0.000001 ? 1 / v : 0) : nu == 2 ? v * 60 / max(tempo, 0.001) : v; );
function au_back(nu, tt) ( nu == 3 ? tt * 1000 : nu == 1 ? (tt > 0.000001 ? 1 / tt : 0) : nu == 2 ? tt * max(tempo, 0.001) / 60 : tt; );
function au_time(off, u, base, nu) local(b, tt, rate) (
  !(u == 1 || (u >= 4 && u <= 9)) || (u == 4 && nu == 3) || (u == 5 && nu == 0) || (u == 1 && nu == 1) || (u == 8 && nu == 2) ? off : (
    b = au_sec(nu, base);
    u == 1 || u == 7 ? (
      rate = (b > 0.000001 ? 1 / b : 0) + (u == 7 ? off / 60 : off);
      tt = rate > 0.000001 ? 1 / rate : 0;
    ) : (
      tt = b + (u == 4 ? off / 1000 : u == 6 ? off * 60 : u == 8 ? off * 60 / max(tempo, 0.001) : u == 9 ? off * max(au_cyc, 0.05) : off);
    );
    au_back(nu, max(0, tt)) - base;
  );
);
// Auto-morph time, in its Rate mode {0 BPM, 1 Seconds, 2 Hz, 3 Every N beats, 4 N per beat}.
// Mine, from the same rules: the amount passes through where its unit is the mode's own, time
// units add to the period, Hz and BPM add to the rate, and a Cycle is the period itself.
function au_rate(off, u, base, mode) local(beat, b, tt, rate) (
  beat = 60 / max(tempo, 0.001);
  !(u == 1 || (u >= 4 && u <= 9)) || (u == 7 && mode == 0) || (u == 5 && mode == 1) || (u == 1 && mode == 2) || (u == 8 && mode == 3) ? off : (
    b = mode == 0 ? 60 / max(base, 0.001) : mode == 1 ? base : mode == 2 ? 1 / max(base, 0.001) : mode == 3 ? base * beat : beat / max(base, 0.001);
    u == 1 || u == 7 ? (
      rate = 1 / max(b, 0.000001) + (u == 7 ? off / 60 : off);
      tt = rate > 0.000001 ? 1 / rate : 1000000;
    ) : (
      tt = b + (u == 4 ? off / 1000 : u == 5 ? off : u == 6 ? off * 60 : u == 8 ? off * beat : off * max(au_cyc, 0.05));
    );
    tt = max(tt, 0.000001);
    (mode == 0 ? 60 / tt : mode == 1 ? tt : mode == 2 ? 1 / tt : mode == 3 ? tt / beat : beat / tt) - base;
  );
);
// Drift + Ramp on one target in its own unit, each amount converted from its own picker. kind
// 1 pitch value (nu = its unit), 2 frequency (nu = the floor in Hz), 3 length (nu = its unit),
// 4 Auto-morph time (nu = Rate mode); anything else takes the amounts as they stand. Both
// pickers on Target default is exactly mo_q, so nothing saved changes.
function au_key(k, kind, base, nu) (
  (target_drift_unit[k] == 0 && ramp_by_unit[k] == 0) || kind < 1 || kind > 4 ? mo_q(k) :
  kind == 1 ? au_pitch(target_drift_offset[k], target_drift_unit[k], base, nu) + au_pitch(ramp_offset_mem[k], ramp_by_unit[k], base, nu) :
  kind == 2 ? au_freq(target_drift_offset[k], target_drift_unit[k], base, nu) + au_freq(ramp_offset_mem[k], ramp_by_unit[k], base, nu) :
  kind == 3 ? au_time(target_drift_offset[k], target_drift_unit[k], base, nu) + au_time(ramp_offset_mem[k], ramp_by_unit[k], base, nu) :
              au_rate(target_drift_offset[k], target_drift_unit[k], base, nu) + au_rate(ramp_offset_mem[k], ramp_by_unit[k], base, nu);
);
// THE DRIFT LOOP RUNS THE OLD TWENTY-FOUR FIRST, IN THEIR OLD ORDER. Random drift
// draws rand(), one stream, so two targets wrapping on one sample must draw in the
// order they always did.
// First the order the 55-target list was visited in (its old twenty-four first), then each of
// those moved to where it sits now, then the thirty-two targets that are new.
mo_k = 0; loop(24, tg_scratch[mo_k] = mo_o2n(mo_k); mo_k += 1;);
mo_i = 0;
loop(55,
  mo_old = 0; mo_j = 0; loop(24, mo_o2n(mo_j) == mo_i ? mo_old = 1; mo_j += 1;);
  !mo_old ? ( tg_scratch[mo_k] = mo_i; mo_k += 1; );
  mo_i += 1;
);
mo_k = 0; loop(55, mo_order[mo_k] = t55_o2n(tg_scratch[mo_k]); mo_k += 1;);
mo_i = 0;
loop(N_TARGETS,
  mo_old = 0; mo_j = 0; loop(55, t55_o2n(mo_j) == mo_i ? mo_old = 1; mo_j += 1;);
  !mo_old ? ( mo_order[mo_k] = mo_i; mo_k += 1; );
  mo_i += 1;
);
drift_ui_inited = 0;   // the Drift/Ramp mirror is adopted in @block
lay_ui_inited = 0;     // ...and so is the layer mirror
// A layer control moved on screen is written to the layer shown, or on All to all sixteen.
function lay_put(c, v, bank) local(lp) (
  v != lay_ui_last[c] ? (
    lay_all ? ( lp = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, bank[lp] = v; lp += 1; ); ) : ( bank[lay_sel] = v; );
    lay_ui_last[c] = v;
  );
);
function lay_adopt() (
  lay_ui_last[0] = slider30; lay_ui_last[1] = slider31; lay_ui_last[2] = slider32;
  lay_ui_last[3] = slider33; lay_ui_last[4] = slider34; lay_ui_last[5] = slider35;
  lay_ui_last[6] = slider36; lay_ui_last[7] = slider37; lay_ui_last[8] = slider38;
);
lpi = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, lay_ot_eff[lpi] = -2; lpi += 1;);

// wavetable sine: linear-interpolated lookup of one precomputed period. Only the
// sin() EVALUATION changes; phase accumulators/increments are untouched, so every
// harmonic stays exactly at (n+1)*f0 -- no detune, no inharmonic content. Phase
// accumulators wrap to [0, TWOPI), so fidx lands in [0, WTBL) and the guard sample
// at sintab[WTBL] covers i0 = WTBL-1.
// Shared play/rest gate, byte-identical to the one in veil.jsfx and
// Full_Feature_Tremolo.jsfx. Ticks EVERY sample whether frozen or not, in
// whatever unit the caller's window is counted in, and returns 1 while resting.
// Off unless BOTH windows are > 0, so a half-configured gate never silently
// freezes anything.
function pr_frozen(accum_bank, resting_bank, idx, tick, play, rest) (
  play > 0 && rest > 0 ? (
    accum_bank[idx] += tick;
    accum_bank[idx] >= (resting_bank[idx] ? rest : play) ? (
      accum_bank[idx] = 0;
      resting_bank[idx] = 1 - resting_bank[idx];
    );
    resting_bank[idx];
  ) : ( 0; );
);

function wtsin(ph) local(fidx, i0, fr) (
  fidx = ph * WTSCALE;
  i0 = floor(fidx); fr = fidx - i0;
  sintab[i0]*(1-fr) + sintab[i0+1]*fr;
);

// grab the most recent MAXFFT input samples into a slot's raw store
function grab_raw(slot) local(i, idx, base) (
  base = slot*MAXFFT;
  i = 0;
  loop(MAXFFT, idx = inpos - MAXFFT + i; idx < 0 ? idx += INRING; slotraw[base+i] = inbuf[idx]; i += 1; );
);

// FFT WA samples of a slot's raw, Hann-windowed, zero-padded to FFTSIZE. Uses the
// FIXED analysis window WA (not the synthesis grain W), so Wash grain never
// triggers this. Capture point picks WHERE in the ~0.68 s grab to look: 0 =
// earliest (the sustained vowel, since you react a beat after hearing it) .. 1 =
// the press-moment.
// Averages the MAGNITUDES of `Capture average` frames stepped through the grab
// by CAPAVG_HOP and centred on the capture point. At 1 frame this is the original
// analysis exactly, so nothing that already exists changes until the dial moves.
//
// Above 1 it fixes the wobble on re-spectralised material: a single frame freezes
// that instant's per-bin scatter and the wash replays it on every grain, so
// roughness that would average out over time instead recurs identically. Natural
// voice is unaffected -- successive frames look alike. Averaging lets the scatter
// cancel while the spectral shape, which every frame agrees on, survives.
//
// Magnitudes, not complex bins: summing complex would let frames cancel by phase,
// which is a comb filter rather than an average. The trade is time-smear, which
// is why it is a dial and not a default.
function compute_spectrum(slot) local(i, f, re, im, mx, rbase, mbase, kk, lo, hi, fstart, fb) (
  kk = max(1, min(CAPAVG_MAX, floor(slot_capavg[slot])));
  lo = slot*MAXFFT;
  rbase = lo + floor(slot_cappoint[slot] * (MAXFFT - WA));   // per-slot capture point
  hi = lo + MAXFFT - WA - (kk-1)*CAPAVG_HOP;                 // last start leaving room for kk frames
  fstart = rbase - floor((kk-1)/2)*CAPAVG_HOP;               // centre the span on the point
  fstart < lo ? fstart = lo;
  fstart > hi ? fstart = hi;
  mbase = slot*NBINS;
  i = 0; loop(NBINS, slotmag[mbase+i] = 0; i += 1; );
  f = 0;
  loop(kk,
    fb = fstart + f*CAPAVG_HOP;
    i = 0; loop(WA, fftwork[2*i] = slotraw[fb+i]*window[i]; fftwork[2*i+1] = 0; i += 1; );
    i = WA; loop(FFTSIZE - WA, fftwork[2*i] = 0; fftwork[2*i+1] = 0; i += 1; );
    fft(fftwork, FFTSIZE); fft_permute(fftwork, FFTSIZE);
    i = 0;
    loop(NBINS,
      re = fftwork[2*i]; im = fftwork[2*i+1];
      slotmag[mbase+i] += sqrt(re*re + im*im);
      i += 1;
    );
    f += 1;
  );
  mx = 0; i = 0;
  loop(NBINS,
    slotmag[mbase+i] = slotmag[mbase+i] / kk;
    slotmag[mbase+i] > mx ? mx = slotmag[mbase+i];
    i += 1;
  );
  slot_norm[slot] = mx > 0 ? mx : 1;
);

// YIN-lite pitch detect (+ subharmonic octave fix) then 64 harmonic magnitudes
// (direct DFT). VOICE end of the Texture knob -- harmonics at the exact detected
// f0 avoid the bin-drift that wrecks phase-coherent resynth from a frozen frame.
function analyze_harm(slot) local(base, ci, almin, almax, tmax, tau, ii, d, dd, run, thr, res, cand, lockon, best, pa, pb, pc, pden, psh, f0d, hn, hf, kk, re, im, ang, hannv, mag, mx, boff) (
  base = slot*MAXFFT + floor(slot_cappoint[slot] * (MAXFFT - WHARM));   // same per-slot point as the wash
  ci = 0; loop(WHARM, anbuf[ci] = slotraw[base+ci]; ci += 1; );
  almin = floor(srate/600); almax = ceil(srate/70); tmax = min(2*almax, WHARM-1);
  tau = 1;
  loop(tmax, d = 0; ii = 0; loop(WHARM - tmax, dd = anbuf[ii] - anbuf[ii+tau]; d += dd*dd; ii += 1; ); yin_df[tau] = d; tau += 1; );
  yin_cmnd[0] = 1; run = 0; tau = 1;
  loop(tmax, run += yin_df[tau]; yin_cmnd[tau] = yin_df[tau]*tau/(run+0.000001); tau += 1; );
  thr = 0.15; res = -1; cand = -1; lockon = 0; tau = almin;
  loop(almax - almin,
    res < 0 ? ( lockon == 0 ? ( yin_cmnd[tau] < thr ? ( lockon = 1; cand = tau; ); )
                            : ( yin_cmnd[tau] < yin_cmnd[cand] ? ( cand = tau; ) : ( res = cand; ); ); );
    tau += 1;
  );
  (res < 0 && lockon == 1) ? res = cand;
  res < 0 ? ( best = 1000000000; tau = almin; loop(almax - almin, yin_cmnd[tau] < best ? ( best = yin_cmnd[tau]; res = tau; ); tau += 1; ); );
  loop(3, (2*res <= tmax && yin_cmnd[2*res] < 0.25 && yin_cmnd[2*res] < 1.15*yin_cmnd[res]) ? res = 2*res; );
  (res > 1 && res < tmax) ? ( pa = yin_cmnd[res-1]; pb = yin_cmnd[res]; pc = yin_cmnd[res+1]; pden = pa-2*pb+pc; psh = (pden!=0)?0.5*(pa-pc)/pden:0; ) : ( psh = 0; );
  f0d = srate/(res+psh);
  (f0d > 20 && f0d < srate*0.5) ? (
    boff = slot*NHARM; mx = 0; hn = 1;
    loop(NHARM,
      hf = hn*f0d;
      hf < srate*0.5 ? (
        re = 0; im = 0; kk = 0;
        loop(WHARM, hannv = 0.5 - 0.5*cos(TWOPI*kk/(WHARM-1)); ang = TWOPI*hf*kk/srate; re += anbuf[kk]*hannv*cos(ang); im -= anbuf[kk]*hannv*sin(ang); kk += 1; );
        mag = sqrt(re*re + im*im);
      ) : ( mag = 0; );
      slot_harm[boff + (hn-1)] = mag; mag > mx ? mx = mag; hn += 1;
    );
    slot_f0[slot] = f0d; slot_hnorm[slot] = mx > 0 ? mx : 1;
  );
);

function capture(slot) (
  n_used == 0 ? analysis_valid = 1;   // the first capture: nothing else is waiting to be analysed
  grab_raw(slot);
  slot_cappoint[slot] = cap_point01;   // stamp this slot with the current capture point; scrubbing later re-tunes only this slot
  slot_capavg[slot]   = cap_avg;       // and the frame count on screen
  compute_spectrum(slot);   // wash (spectral) analysis
  analyze_harm(slot);       // voice (harmonic) analysis -- same captured frame
  have = 1;
  (slot + 1) > n_used ? n_used = slot + 1;
);

// Per-layer Overtone on the wash is applied INLINE in the bin loop below, at the
// point each layer reads the captured spectrum. It is written out twice rather
// than put in a function on purpose: this loop runs FFTSIZE/2+1 times, times
// every audible layer, twice per grain, and it is the hottest loop in the file.
//
// The three rules match the voice curve: scale by that layer's power
// normalisation everywhere, lift inside the window, and never lift below the
// fundamental's own peak. Away from the window the factor is JUST the
// normalisation, so the range test skips all the window arithmetic for the
// overwhelming majority of bins -- one compare and one multiply.
function build_spectrum(useR) local(i, srcb, b0, fr, m, ph, woff, re, im, mir, lq, mb, hcw, lm, od, ow) (
  bsum_base = 0; bsum_all = 0;   // per-call totals, read by gen_grain after the L pass
  woff = eff_stereo_width * 0.01 * PI;   // eff_ = base + Drift offset (set per-sample)
  // Build a Hermitian-symmetric spectrum so ifft() returns a REAL signal:
  // fill bins 0..N/2 from the magnitude data, mirror them as conjugates.
  i = 0;
  loop(FFTSIZE/2 + 1,
    srcb = i / lpr[LAY_ORIG];   // Layer 1 moves like any layer
    (srcb >= 0 && srcb < NBINS - 1) ? (
      b0 = floor(srcb); fr = srcb - b0; m = curmag[b0]*(1-fr) + curmag[b0+1]*fr;
      // The Original is a layer like any other, so it takes LAY_ORIG's overtone.
      ot_wash ? (
        (srcb > ot_f0bin && abs(srcb - lot_bin[LAY_ORIG]) < ot_binw) ? (
          od = abs(srcb - lot_bin[LAY_ORIG]) / ot_binw;
          ow = 0.5 + 0.5*wtsin(od*PI + PI*0.5);
          m = m * (1 + (ot_boost - 1)*ow) * lay_ot_norm[LAY_ORIG];
        ) : (
          m = m * lay_ot_norm[LAY_ORIG];
        );
      );
    ) : ( m = 0; );
    // Layers on the wash: the same magnitude spectrum read again at each layer's
    // own bin scale and summed in. This is why layers are nearly free here --
    // one extra interpolated read per bin, inside a grain that was already going
    // to be built, rather than a second pair of FFTs per layer. They share this
    // bin's random phase, which is exactly right for a phase-randomised bed:
    // where two layers land on one bin they are noise either way.
    m = m * base_gain;          // the original's own level: its gain, or 0 when something else is soloed
    nlay_on ? (
      mb = m;                     // keep the un-layered magnitude for the auto-gain below
      lq = 0;
      loop(N_LAY_SLOTS,
        // LAY_ORIG is the original: it is already in m above, via the base
        // lookup, so the ladder skips it rather than adding it twice.
        (lq != LAY_ORIG && layer_gain[lq] > 0.0001) ? (
          srcb = i / lpr[lq];
          (srcb >= 0 && srcb < NBINS - 1) ? (
            b0 = floor(srcb); fr = srcb - b0;
            lm = curmag[b0]*(1-fr) + curmag[b0+1]*fr;
            ot_wash ? (
              (srcb > ot_f0bin && abs(srcb - lot_bin[lq]) < ot_binw) ? (
                od = abs(srcb - lot_bin[lq]) / ot_binw;
                ow = 0.5 + 0.5*wtsin(od*PI + PI*0.5);
                lm = lm * (1 + (ot_boost - 1)*ow) * lay_ot_norm[lq];
              ) : (
                lm = lm * lay_ot_norm[lq];
              );
            );
            m += layer_gain[lq] * lm;
          );
        );
        lq += 1;
      );
    );
    // High cut, here in the OUTPUT bin domain -- i is an absolute frequency, so
    // the cut stays where it was set while Pitch and the layers move under it.
    hcw = 1;
    hc_on ? (
      i >= hcbin_hi ? ( hcw = 0; ) : i > hcbin_lo ? ( hcw = (hcbin_hi - i) * hcbin_inv; );
      m = m * hcw;
    );
    // Running totals with and without the layers. The wash is normalised per
    // grain to a fixed RMS, which would otherwise duck the base every time a
    // layer came in -- the same "the plugin keeps re-mixing you" problem the
    // voice had, but hidden inside an auto-gain nobody asked to be part of the
    // conversation. Parseval says grain RMS goes as sqrt(sum of squares), so
    // the ratio of these two IS the amount the layers inflated it, and dividing
    // it back out in gen_grain leaves the base sitting exactly where it sat.
    nlay_on ? ( mb = mb*hcw; bsum_base += mb*mb; bsum_all += m*m; );
    ph = phase[i] + (useR ? woff*doff[i] : 0);
    re = m * cos(ph); im = m * sin(ph);
    fftwork[2*i] = re; fftwork[2*i+1] = im;
    (i > 0 && i < FFTSIZE/2) ? ( mir = FFTSIZE - i; fftwork[2*mir] = re; fftwork[2*mir+1] = -im; );
    i += 1;
  );
  fftwork[1] = 0;                       // DC imaginary = 0
  fftwork[2*(FFTSIZE/2)+1] = 0;         // Nyquist imaginary = 0
  fft_ipermute(fftwork, FFTSIZE);
  ifft(fftwork, FFTSIZE);
);

function gen_grain() local(i, j, si, sj, mfr, ni, nj, bi, bj, sp, sumv, cnt, bj2, pk, thr, cutbin, sv, rms, gnorm, aw, lq2,
                          npc, pk2, pst, pn, pw, bsb, bsa) (
  // slot selection (vsi/vsj/vmfr) is computed once per sample in @sample so the
  // wash and the harmonic voice always agree on which slot(s) they're playing.
  si = vsi; sj = vsj; mfr = vmfr;
  ni = slot_norm[si]; ni <= 0 ? ni = 1;
  nj = slot_norm[sj]; nj <= 0 ? nj = 1;
  bi = si*NBINS; bj = sj*NBINS;
  i = 0;
  loop(NBINS, mtmp[i] = (1-mfr)*(slotmag[bi+i]/ni) + mfr*(slotmag[bj+i]/nj); i += 1; );
  sp = floor(eff_spread_hz * FFTSIZE / srate);   // eff_ = base + Drift offset
  // Spread's box blur as a RUNNING TOTAL, Passage's version (2026-09-12). The counted
  // version re-added every neighbour for every bin, so its cost grew with the width --
  // with Spread now reaching 1000 Hz that is about seven times Spread 150's work per
  // grain. Entering bin i, add the neighbour that came into range and drop the one that
  // left; the window is clipped at both ends and divided by its real width, as before.
  i = 0;
  sp > 0 ? (
    sumv = 0; cnt = 0; j = 0;
    loop(min(sp, NBINS - 1) + 1, sumv += mtmp[j]; cnt += 1; j += 1; );
  );
  loop(NBINS,
    sp <= 0 ? ( curmag[i] = mtmp[i]; ) : (
      curmag[i] = sumv / cnt;
      bj2 = i + 1 + sp; bj2 < NBINS ? ( sumv += mtmp[bj2]; cnt += 1; );
      bj2 = i - sp;     bj2 >= 0    ? ( sumv -= mtmp[bj2]; cnt -= 1; );
    );
    i += 1;
  );
  ot_wash = (ot_on && ot_bin > 0) ? 1 : 0;
  pk = 0; i = 0; loop(NBINS, curmag[i] > pk ? pk = curmag[i]; i += 1; );
  thr = denoise_amt * 0.01 * 0.1 * pk;
  cutbin = floor(eff_lowcut_hz * FFTSIZE / srate);   // eff_ = base + Drift offset
  i = 0;
  loop(NBINS,
    i < cutbin ? ( curmag[i] = 0; ) : ( curmag[i] = max(0, curmag[i] - thr); );
    i += 1;
  );
  // Overtone on the wash is NOT applied here any more. It used to be folded
  // into curmag once, which made it one setting for the whole instrument --
  // every layer reads this same array, so every layer got the same harmonic.
  // It now happens inside build_spectrum, per layer, at the point each layer
  // reads the spectrum, so `Layer overtone harmonic` reaches the wash as well
  // as the voice. curmag stays the clean captured spectrum.
  //
  // The move also corrects the domain. The centre was computed as f0 x harmonic
  // x pitch_ratio -- an OUTPUT bin -- and then used to index curmag, which is in
  // SOURCE bins. With Pitch at 0 the two coincide and it was right; at any other
  // Pitch the wash lifted harmonic n x pitch_ratio instead of harmonic n. Found
  // by reading on 2026-09-01, NOT yet confirmed by ear.
  // Per-grain derivations for the two things build_spectrum needs beyond curmag:
  // where the High cut sits in output bins, and each layer's source-bin scale.
  // Both are read from the per-sample globals as they stand at grain time --
  // a grain is a snapshot, which is why Drift on either is smooth rather than
  // stepped: the steps are one grain apart and the grains overlap four deep.
  hcbin_hi  = hc_on ? hc_top * FFTSIZE / srate : NBINS + 1;
  hcbin_lo  = hc_on ? hc_lo  * FFTSIZE / srate : NBINS + 1;
  hcbin_inv = hc_on ? 1 / max(1, hcbin_hi - hcbin_lo) : 0;
  lq2 = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, lpr[lq2] = pitch_ratio * layer_ratio[lq2]; lq2 += 1; );
  W <= FFTSIZE ? (
  // --- A grain that fits one FFTSIZE piece: the original build, unchanged. ---
  i = 0; loop(NBINS, phase[i] = rand(1)*TWOPI; i += 1; );
  build_spectrum(0);
  rms = 0; i = 0; loop(W, sv = fftwork[2*i]; grainbuf[i] = sv; rms += sv*sv; i += 1; );
  rms = sqrt(rms / W);
  // Take the layers back out of the level the auto-gain is measuring, so the
  // base sits where it would with no layers at all and each layer arrives at
  // exactly the dB it was given. Without this the auto-gain reads the fuller
  // spectrum as "too loud" and quietly pulls everything down around it.
  (nlay_on && bsum_base > 0 && bsum_all > 0) ? ( rms = rms * sqrt(bsum_base / bsum_all); );
  // Slow auto-gain: smooth the grain RMS across grains so loudness is stable and
  // does NOT pump per grain. With the sqrt-Hann constant-power window below,
  // overlapping random-phase grains sum to steady power. Target ~0.07 grain RMS
  // -> ~0.1 output RMS (-20 dBFS). Freeze the smoother on silent grains (empty
  // slot) so switching back doesn't blip loud.
  rms > 0.0000001 ? ( rms_smooth <= 0 ? rms_smooth = rms : rms_smooth += 0.02 * (rms - rms_smooth); );
  gnorm = rms_smooth > 0.0000001 ? (0.07 / rms_smooth) : 0;
  build_spectrum(1);
  i = 0;
  loop(W,
    aw = accpos + i; aw >= ACCLEN ? aw -= ACCLEN;
    accL[aw] += grainbuf[i]   * gnorm * synwin[i];
    accR[aw] += fftwork[2*i]  * gnorm * synwin[i];
    i += 1;
  );
  ) : (
  // --- A grain longer than FFTSIZE (2026-09-13): FFTSIZE pieces every half piece, each
  //     with fresh random phases, summed under pwin. The first piece is not faded in and
  //     the last not faded out -- synwin already shapes the grain's two ends. Pieces are
  //     independent noise with the same spectrum, so constant power across an overlap is
  //     a constant level. The auto-gain then measures the assembled grain as before. ---
  npc = ceil((W - FFTSIZE) / (FFTSIZE/2)) + 1;
  i = 0; loop(W, grainbuf[i] = 0; grainR[i] = 0; i += 1; );
  bsb = 0; bsa = 0; pk2 = 0;
  loop(npc,
    pst = pk2 * FFTSIZE/2;
    pn = min(FFTSIZE, W - pst);
    i = 0; loop(NBINS, phase[i] = rand(1)*TWOPI; i += 1; );
    build_spectrum(0);
    bsb += bsum_base; bsa += bsum_all;
    j = 0;
    loop(pn,
      pw = ((pk2 == 0 && j < FFTSIZE/2) || (pk2 == npc - 1 && j >= FFTSIZE/2)) ? 1 : pwin[j];
      grainbuf[pst + j] += fftwork[2*j] * pw;
      j += 1;
    );
    build_spectrum(1);
    j = 0;
    loop(pn,
      pw = ((pk2 == 0 && j < FFTSIZE/2) || (pk2 == npc - 1 && j >= FFTSIZE/2)) ? 1 : pwin[j];
      grainR[pst + j] += fftwork[2*j] * pw;
      j += 1;
    );
    pk2 += 1;
  );
  rms = 0; i = 0; loop(W, sv = grainbuf[i]; rms += sv*sv; i += 1; );
  rms = sqrt(rms / W);
  (nlay_on && bsb > 0 && bsa > 0) ? ( rms = rms * sqrt(bsb / bsa); );
  rms > 0.0000001 ? ( rms_smooth <= 0 ? rms_smooth = rms : rms_smooth += 0.02 * (rms - rms_smooth); );
  gnorm = rms_smooth > 0.0000001 ? (0.07 / rms_smooth) : 0;
  i = 0;
  loop(W,
    aw = accpos + i; aw >= ACCLEN ? aw -= ACCLEN;
    accL[aw] += grainbuf[i] * gnorm * synwin[i];
    accR[aw] += grainR[i]   * gnorm * synwin[i];
    i += 1;
  );
  );
);

// Rebuild ONLY the synthesis grain window (sqrt-Hann, length W = Wash grain).
// CHEAP: no FFT, no YIN, no accumulator clear -- so Wash grain can be dragged
// or AUTOMATED without dropouts (old-length grains finish as new ones start).
function build_synwin(wlen) local(i) (
  wlen = max(64, min(GMAX, floor(wlen)));   // was MAXFFT: see GMAX
  W = wlen; HOP = max(1, floor(W / 4));
  i = 0; loop(W, synwin[i] = sqrt(0.5 - 0.5*cos(TWOPI*i/(W-1))); i += 1; );
);
function clear_accum() local(i) (
  i = 0; loop(ACCLEN, accL[i] = 0; accR[i] = 0; i += 1; );
  accpos = 0; hopcount = 0;
);

// Fisher-Yates shuffle of the first n_used slot indices into driftbag. Drift
// draws its next slot from this bag, so it visits every captured slot exactly
// once per cycle (even coverage, random order) rather than a memoryless walk
// that clusters in the middle. Avoids an immediate repeat across the reshuffle
// seam (if the new first slot equals the one just visited, swap it back one).
// Reorder one bank in place: dst[q] = src[tbl[q]], over N_LAY_SLOTS entries
// starting at pb_off. Used only by the @serialize migration.
function permute_bank(pb_base, pb_off, pb_tbl) local(pb_q) (
  pb_q = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, ser_scratch[pb_q] = pb_base[pb_off + pb_q]; pb_q += 1; );
  pb_q = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, pb_base[pb_off + pb_q] = ser_scratch[pb_tbl[pb_q]]; pb_q += 1; );
);

function shuffle_bag() local(i, j, t) (
  i = 0; loop(n_used, driftbag[i] = i; i += 1; );
  i = n_used - 1;
  loop(n_used - 1,
    j = floor(rand(i + 1));
    t = driftbag[i]; driftbag[i] = driftbag[j]; driftbag[j] = t;
    i -= 1;
  );
  (n_used > 1 && driftbag[0] == last_slot) ? ( t = driftbag[0]; driftbag[0] = driftbag[1]; driftbag[1] = t; );
);

@slider
// Every slider read into a named global here; nothing downstream references a
// sliderN directly, so the layout can be reordered without desyncing the engine.

// --- Rate Mode: convert the time values when the mode actually moves.
//     Same shape as Womb's Rate Mode conversion, which is the ear-tested one.
//     Gated on `time_mode_inited`, which is adopted in @block and NOT here:
//     on a fresh or duplicated instance @slider runs while REAPER is still
//     handing over saved values, so a tracker adopted here would capture a
//     DEFAULT and then read the real value arriving as a user edit -- which
//     is exactly the bug that stamped 0.5 over a hand-set rate on every load
//     (2026-08-23). While the flag is still 0 this fires nothing at all, and
//     that is the safe direction. `sliderchange`, not `slider_automate`: this
//     is a unit conversion, not something to write into automation.
//     Three units now, so the conversion goes THROUGH SECONDS rather than
//     being a single ratio: whatever we were in, turn it into seconds; then
//     turn seconds into whatever we are going to. One table, no pairwise cases,
//     and adding a fourth unit later needs one line rather than n-squared.
time_mode_inited && last_time_mode != slider9 ? (
  // Auto-morph time is a PERIOD, so each mode is "seconds per unit of it":
  //   BPM (0)      v cycles a minute -> 60/v seconds, so it INVERTS
  //   Seconds (1)  v seconds, identity
  //   Hz (2)       v cycles a second -> 1/v seconds, inverts
  //   Host x (3)   v beats -> v * one beat in seconds
  // The inverting pair cannot be expressed as a single ratio, so the conversion
  // goes to seconds and back rather than pairing units off.
  tm_beat_sec = 60 / max(tempo, 0.001);
  tm_secs     = last_time_mode == 0 ? 60 / max(slider8, 0.001) :
                last_time_mode == 1 ? slider8 :
                last_time_mode == 2 ? 1 / max(slider8, 0.001) :
                last_time_mode == 3 ? slider8 * tm_beat_sec :
                                      tm_beat_sec / max(slider8, 0.001);
  slider8     = slider9 == 0 ? 60 / max(tm_secs, 0.001) :
                slider9 == 1 ? tm_secs :
                slider9 == 2 ? 1 / max(tm_secs, 0.001) :
                slider9 == 3 ? tm_secs / tm_beat_sec :
                               tm_beat_sec / max(tm_secs, 0.001);
  slider8     = min(1000, max(0.01, slider8));
  // The transport times and Drift period are DURATIONS and cannot be in BPM or
  // Hz -- a start delay of "4 Hz" is not a thing. They read as seconds, or as
  // beats in Host x, which is the same shape the suite uses for every control
  // whose meaning changes only under sync. So their conversion depends on
  // whether we crossed the Host x boundary, not on which of the other three.
  // >= 3: the durations read as BEATS in either host mode -- a start delay is a
  // length of time, and 'per beat' is not a length, so both sync modes use the
  // same beats reading.
  tr_from     = last_time_mode >= 3 ? tm_beat_sec : 1;
  tr_to       = slider9        >= 3 ? tm_beat_sec : 1;
  sync_k      = tr_from / tr_to;
  // The transport times, added 2026-09-04, convert on the same flip and for the
  // same reason: the number on screen keeps meaning the same LENGTH OF TIME, and
  // only the unit you type in changes. Their floor is 0 rather than 0.01 because
  // 0 is the OFF value for all three and must survive a flip untouched -- a
  // conversion that turned "no start delay" into a small one would be exactly
  // the silent value change this suite forbids.
  slider41 = slider41 > 0 ? min(1000, max(0.001, slider41 * sync_k)) : 0;
  slider42 = slider42 > 0 ? min(1000, max(0.001, slider42 * sync_k)) : 0;
  slider43 = slider43 > 0 ? min(1000, max(0.001, slider43 * sync_k)) : 0;
  // Drift period is NO LONGER converted here. It has its own unit control
  // (slider51) since 2026-09-06, so Rate Mode has nothing to say about it: the
  // number means whatever `Drift period unit` says and Rate Mode leaves it
  // alone. Removing the conversion is safe on real data -- every one of the 122
  // instances in the library is on Rate Mode = Seconds, so this line has never
  // once fired -- and keeping it would have been the forbidden thing: one
  // control silently rewriting another that now names its own unit on itself.
  sliderchange(-1);
  last_time_mode = slider9;
);

// All slots, 2026-09-11 (docs/layouts/spectral-vowel-morpher-all-slots-20260911.md):
// index 0 is All, and 1-8 are the slots they always were, so no saved value moves.
cap_sel       = max(0, min(NSLOTS, floor(slider1 + 0.5)));   // 0 = All, 1-8 = Slot 1-8
cap_all       = cap_sel == 0;
cap_slot      = cap_all ? 0 : cap_sel - 1;             // engine is 0-7; All shows and auditions Slot 1
cap_trig      = slider2; first_slider ? ( cap_last = cap_trig; first_slider = 0; );  // first @slider after load: sync cap_last so a saved-ON Capture-now never fires a silent capture on reload
// --- Per-slot Capture point (nested-selector, like Drift/Ramp targets). The
//     Capture-point slider is now PER SLOT: switching Capture slot saves the
//     old slot's point and loads the new slot's stored point into slider3, so
//     each slot keeps its own by-ear tuning. Editing slider3 writes the current
//     slot. Suppress the @block re-derive on a pure slot-switch (each slot is
//     already analyzed at its own point) by syncing last_cappoint. ---
last_cap_sel != cap_sel ? (
  !last_cap_all ? slot_cappoint[last_cap_slot] = slider3 * 0.01;   // save the slot we're leaving; All holds nothing of its own
  slider3 = slot_cappoint[cap_slot] * 100;         // load the slot we're entering (All shows Slot 1's)
  slider_automate(slider3);
  last_cap_slot = cap_slot; last_cap_sel = cap_sel; last_cap_all = cap_all;
  last_cappoint = slot_cappoint[cap_slot];          // no re-derive on switch: slot already analyzed at this point
  cp_ui_last = slider3;
) : cap_all ? (
  // On All, a Capture point edit reaches all eight. Change-detected on Polyrhythm's
  // model, so parking on All -- or a stray @slider pass on load -- flattens nothing.
  slider3 != cp_ui_last ? (
    cpa = 0; loop(NSLOTS, slot_cappoint[cpa] = slider3 * 0.01; cpa += 1; );
    cp_ui_last = slider3; cap_all_scrub = 1;
  );
) : (
  slot_cappoint[cap_slot] = slider3 * 0.01;         // capture a live scrub of the current slot
  cp_ui_last = slider3;
);
cap_point01   = slider3 * 0.01;
// Capture average is GLOBAL: one slider value stamped onto every slot, so
// switching slots neither saves nor loads it and there is nothing per-slot left
// to get out of step. The per-slot bank stays as the storage compute_spectrum
// reads and as the @serialize field (byte format unchanged) -- it just never
// holds eight different values any more. Stamping every @slider is also what
// flattens a project that was saved back when this WAS per slot.
cap_avg       = max(1, min(CAPAVG_MAX, slider4));
sca2 = 0; loop(NSLOTS, slot_capavg[sca2] = cap_avg; sca2 += 1; );
// Overtone is GLOBAL in this plugin -- see the slider block.
ot_harm_base  = max(0, min(NHARM, slider26));
ot_depth_base = max(0, min(48, slider27));    // bases for Drift/Ramp targets 11 and 12
ot_width_base = max(0.5, min(4, slider28));
ot_depth_db   = ot_depth_base;
ot_width      = ot_width_base;
// --- Layers (2026-09-13): All, then Layer 1-16; Layer 1 is the Original. Every layer
//     sounds in parallel from @sample; the selector is a view, never a mute. Change-
//     detected on Polyrhythm v3's model: nothing is saved on a switch because every edit is
//     written as it happens, only a control actually moved is written, and on All it
//     reaches all sixteen. Nothing is written until @block or @serialize has adopted what
//     is on screen. ---
lay_selv = max(0, min(N_LAY_SLOTS, floor(slider29 + 0.5)));   // 0 = All, 1-16 = Layer 1-16
lay_all  = lay_selv == 0;
lay_sel  = lay_all ? 0 : lay_selv - 1;                         // All shows Layer 1
lay_ui_inited ? (
  last_lay_selv != lay_selv ? (
    slider30 = lay_active[lay_sel];  slider_automate(slider30);
    slider31 = lay_semi[lay_sel];    slider_automate(slider31);
    slider32 = lay_punit[lay_sel];   slider_automate(slider32);
    slider33 = lay_fine[lay_sel];    slider_automate(slider33);
    slider34 = lay_funit[lay_sel];   slider_automate(slider34);
    slider35 = lay_db_base[lay_sel]; slider_automate(slider35);
    slider36 = lay_solo[lay_sel];    slider_automate(slider36);
    slider37 = lay_nharm[lay_sel];   slider_automate(slider37);
    slider38 = lay_ot_harm[lay_sel]; slider_automate(slider38);
    lay_adopt();
    last_lay_sel = lay_sel; last_lay_selv = lay_selv;
  ) : (
    lay_put(0, slider30, lay_active);
    lay_put(1, slider31, lay_semi);
    lay_put(2, slider32, lay_punit);
    lay_put(3, slider33, lay_fine);
    lay_put(4, slider34, lay_funit);
    lay_put(5, slider35, lay_db_base);
    lay_put(6, slider36, lay_solo);
    lay_put(7, slider37, lay_nharm);
    lay_put(8, slider38, lay_ot_harm);
  );
);
// Layer ratios and gains are DERIVED from the banks, so they are computed in
// @block, not here -- see the note there. Deriving them in @slider is what
// broke restore: @slider and @serialize have no guaranteed relative order.
dry_gain      = slider39 <= -60 ? 0 : pow(10, slider39/20);   // -60 = true silence
dry_db_base   = slider39;                                    // base for Input level drift
voice_db_base = slider40;                                    // base for Voice level drift
voice_gain    = slider40 <= -60 ? 0 : pow(10, slider40/20);
texture       = slider10 * 0.01;                              // 0 voice .. 1 wash
hlevel        = cos(texture * PI * 0.5);                     // equal-power crossfade
wlevel        = sin(texture * PI * 0.5);
grain_ms        = slider11;
spread_hz     = slider12;
// --- The pitch block. Tuning reference is heard only where a value is in Hz.
tuning_base = slider21 >= 20 ? slider21 : 440; tuning_ref = tuning_base;
// Source note and Target note mirror Transpose value -- Bubbler's design -- only while a
// Source note is set and Transpose is in Semitones. Source note and its fine tune never
// change the sound: moving them re-reads the Target note (Rozaya: "The source note is just
// to tell the targget what 0 semitones is though"). Picking a Target note writes the shift.
// Nothing fires until @block has adopted the trackers.
(pv_inited && slider13 > 0 && slider18 == 1) ? (
  pv_src = (slider13 - 1) + pv_src_semis(slider13, slider15, slider14);
  slider16 != pv_last_tgt ? (
    slider17 = slider16 - pv_src; slider_automate(slider17);
  ) : (slider17 != pv_last_tval || slider13 != pv_last_src || slider14 != pv_last_sf || slider15 != pv_last_sfu) ? (
    slider16 = max(0, min(127, floor(slider17 + pv_src + 0.5))); slider_automate(slider16);
  );
);
slider_show(slider16, slider13 > 0 && slider18 == 1);
pv_last_tgt = slider16; pv_last_tval = slider17; pv_last_src = slider13; pv_last_sf = slider14; pv_last_sfu = slider15;
pitch_semi    = slider17;                                    // Transpose value, base for its drift
pitch_unit    = slider18;
pitch_fine    = slider19;
pitch_funit   = slider20;
// At Semitones with no fine tune this is the same number it always was.
pitch_ratio   = pow(2.0, (pv_semis(pitch_unit, pitch_semi) + pv_semis(pitch_funit, pitch_fine))/12);
stereo_width  = slider22;
// Clamped to 100 to match `eff_stereo_width` in @sample. These are the two
// consumers of Stereo width, and until 2026-09-06 only ONE of them clamped:
// the wash saturated at 100 while the voice detune scaled on past it -- but
// only when no drift or ramp targeted this control, because the @sample line
// recomputes it from the clamped value when one does. Same control, three
// different behaviours depending on the texture and on whether drift was
// configured. Now one.
voice_detune  = min(100, stereo_width) * 0.01 * VOICE_DETUNE_MAX;   // voice width via detuned L/R unison
lowcut_hz     = slider24;
hicut_hz      = slider25;                                    // base for High cut drift (20000 = off)
denoise_amt   = slider23;
denoise_base  = slider23;                                    // base for Denoise drift
audition_mode = slider5;                                   // 0 focused slot, 1 morph
morph01       = slider6 * 0.01;
automorph_mode = slider7;
// Auto-morph time. The RAW slider is read here; the seconds it actually means
// are derived in @block, because under sync it follows the project tempo and a
// tempo change does not re-run @slider.
time_mode     = slider9;        // 0 BPM, 1 Seconds, 2 Hz, 3 Host x
auto_time_raw = max(0.01, slider8);
automorph_mode != last_automode ? (
  last_automode = automorph_mode; morph_phase = 0;
  drift_primed = 0;   // force Drift to (re)build its shuffle bag on the next sample
);
// ALWAYS VISIBLE since 2026-09-04. It used to hide unless Auto-morph was on,
// because Auto-morph time was the only thing it governed. It now also decides
// whether Start delay, Play for, Rest for and Drift period read as seconds or
// beats, so hiding it would leave five controls whose unit is set by a mode
// you cannot see -- which is the exact failure R10 exists to stop.
slider_show(slider9, 1);
slider_show(slider8, automorph_mode > 0.5);

// --- Drift target selector save/restore (nested-selector pattern, matching the
//     rest of the suite). Switching the target saves the four amount/period/shape
//     sliders into the OLD
//     target's bank slot, then loads the NEW target's stored config into the
//     visible sliders. Otherwise it captures live edits to the current target.
//     All targets drift in parallel from @sample regardless of what's selected. ---
// Change-detected since 2026-09-11, Polyrhythm's shape: an "all layers" entry
// writes into sixteen targets, so a wholesale capture on a stray @slider pass would
// flatten them. No save-on-switch step -- every edit is written as it happens.
// Nothing is written until @block has adopted the mirror.
sel_target = slider46;
drift_ui_inited ? (
  last_target_select != sel_target ? (
    dts = mo_read_slot(sel_target);
    slider47 = target_drift_up[dts];
    slider48 = target_drift_down[dts];
    slider50 = target_drift_per[dts];
    slider52 = target_drift_shape[dts];
    slider53 = target_drift_play[dts];
    slider54 = target_drift_rest[dts];
    slider49 = target_drift_unit[dts];
    slider_automate(slider49);
    slider_automate(slider47);
    slider_automate(slider48);
    slider_automate(slider50);
    slider_automate(slider52);
    slider_automate(slider53);
    slider_automate(slider54);
    dui_last[0] = slider47; dui_last[1] = slider48; dui_last[2] = slider50;
    dui_last[3] = slider52; dui_last[4] = slider53; dui_last[5] = slider54;
    dui_last[11] = slider49;
    last_target_select = sel_target;
  ) : (
    mo_capture(sel_target, target_drift_up,    slider47, 0);
    mo_capture(sel_target, target_drift_down,  slider48, 1);
    mo_capture(sel_target, target_drift_per,   slider50, 2);
    mo_capture(sel_target, target_drift_shape, slider52, 3);
    mo_capture(sel_target, target_drift_play,  slider53, 4);
    mo_capture(sel_target, target_drift_rest,  slider54, 5);
    mo_capture(sel_target, target_drift_unit,  slider49, 11);
  );
);
// Per-target "is this target being driven at all?" flag — lets @sample skip the
// per-sample effective-value recompute (and keep the exact original CPU/output)
// for any target with no drift configured.
// --- Ramp target selector save/restore (nested-selector, mirrors Drift).
//     Ramp target selects; `by`, duration and start delay are per target.
//     Ramp engage is GLOBAL. All targets ride in parallel from @sample. ---
rsel_target = slider56;
drift_ui_inited ? (
  last_ramp_target != rsel_target ? (
    rts = mo_read_slot(rsel_target);
    slider57 = ramp_by_mem[rts];
    slider60 = ramp_dur_mem[rts];
    slider64 = ramp_delay_mem[rts];
    slider61 = ramp_play_mem[rts];
    slider62 = ramp_rest_mem[rts];
    slider58 = ramp_by_unit[rts];
    slider_automate(slider58);
    slider_automate(slider57);
    slider_automate(slider60);
    slider_automate(slider64);
    slider_automate(slider61);
    slider_automate(slider62);
    dui_last[6] = slider57; dui_last[7] = slider60; dui_last[8] = slider64;
    dui_last[9] = slider61; dui_last[10] = slider62;
    dui_last[12] = slider58;
    last_ramp_target = rsel_target;
  ) : (
    mo_capture(rsel_target, ramp_by_mem,    slider57, 6);
    mo_capture(rsel_target, ramp_dur_mem,   slider60, 7);
    mo_capture(rsel_target, ramp_delay_mem, slider64, 8);
    mo_capture(rsel_target, ramp_play_mem,  slider61, 9);
    mo_capture(rsel_target, ramp_rest_mem,  slider62, 10);
    mo_capture(rsel_target, ramp_by_unit,   slider58, 12);
  );
);
ramp_engaged = slider63;

// td_active / mod_active are derived from the drift and ramp banks, so they are
// computed in @block too, for exactly the same reason.
// eff_ defaults = base (so the first @sample and any inactive target read base).
eff_spread_hz = spread_hz; eff_lowcut_hz = lowcut_hz; eff_stereo_width = stereo_width;
drift_restart_free = slider55;   // 0 = restart on play (sync), 1 = free-running

@block
// Adopt the Sync-to-host tracker here rather than in @slider -- see the note
// on the conversion block in @slider for why. @block cannot run before the
// instance is configured, so this adopts a settled value whatever order the
// restore paths ran in.
!time_mode_inited ? ( last_time_mode = slider9; time_mode_inited = 1; );
!lay_ui_inited ? (
  lay_selv = max(0, min(N_LAY_SLOTS, floor(slider29 + 0.5)));
  last_lay_selv = lay_selv; last_lay_sel = lay_selv == 0 ? 0 : lay_selv - 1;
  lay_adopt(); lay_ui_inited = 1;
);
!pv_inited ? ( pv_last_tgt = slider16; pv_last_tval = slider17; pv_last_src = slider13; pv_last_sf = slider14; pv_last_sfu = slider15; pv_inited = 1; );
// The Drift/Ramp mirror, adopted HERE and never in @slider (2026-09-11).
!drift_ui_inited ? (
  dui_last[0] = slider47; dui_last[1] = slider48; dui_last[2] = slider50;
  dui_last[3] = slider52; dui_last[4] = slider53; dui_last[5] = slider54;
  dui_last[6] = slider57; dui_last[7] = slider60; dui_last[8] = slider64;
  dui_last[9] = slider61; dui_last[10] = slider62;
  dui_last[11] = slider49; dui_last[12] = slider58;
  drift_ui_inited = 1;
);
// Auto-morph time in SECONDS, derived every block. Under sync the slider is a
// beat count, so this is where the project tempo enters; @slider would go
// stale the moment the tempo changed, since a tempo change does not re-run it.
// Auto-morph PERIOD in seconds. Seconds mode is the identity; the other four
// are one line each. Derived per block because the host modes read the live
// tempo and @slider does not re-run on a tempo change.
//
// R21, and this is a CONVERSION, not a gate: the two host modes are RECIPROCALS
// of each other and each needs its own formula. `Every N beats` (3) is N beats
// long; `N per beat` (4) is one beat divided N ways. This chain ended at mode 3
// and let mode 4 fall into it, so "8 per beat" ran as "every 8 beats" -- 64x too
// slow at 120 BPM, silently. Same defect as c4c9b31 (Tremolo, Sweeping Filter)
// and 47da263 (Resonance Bank); this plugin was missed by that sweep. Latent --
// no instance in the library has ever been in either host mode -- but `Cycles`
// in the Drift and Ramp unit controls counts THIS period, so it had to be right
// before those could be. (Fixed 2026-09-06.)
//
// Both host arms divide by auto_time_raw only where it is the divisor, and
// auto_time_raw is already clamped to >= 0.01 at its @slider read, so neither
// arm can divide by zero.
// Auto-morph time (target 1), in the slider's own unit, while driven.
// One Auto-morph traversal without its own drift: what a Cycle is in the amount units.
au_cyc = max(0.01,
  time_mode == 0 ? 60 / max(auto_time_raw, 0.001) :
  time_mode == 1 ? auto_time_raw :
  time_mode == 2 ? 1 / max(auto_time_raw, 0.001) :
  time_mode == 3 ? auto_time_raw * 60 / max(tempo, 0.001) :
                   (60 / max(tempo, 0.001)) / auto_time_raw);
mo_at_raw = mod_active[1] ? max(0.01, auto_time_raw + au_key(1, 4, auto_time_raw, time_mode)) : auto_time_raw;
auto_time = max(0.01,
  time_mode == 0 ? 60 / max(mo_at_raw, 0.001) :
  time_mode == 1 ? mo_at_raw :
  time_mode == 2 ? 1 / max(mo_at_raw, 0.001) :
  time_mode == 3 ? mo_at_raw * 60 / max(tempo, 0.001) :
                   (60 / max(tempo, 0.001)) / mo_at_raw);
// Transport times in SECONDS, derived here for the same reason auto_time is:
// under sync the sliders are beat counts, so this is where the project tempo
// enters, and @slider would go stale the moment the tempo changed because a
// tempo change does not re-run it. beats -> seconds is beats * 60 / tempo.
// Seconds per unit for the DURATIONS -- Start delay, Play for, Rest for and
// Drift period. These are times, not rates, so they cannot be in BPM or Hz: they
// read as seconds, or as beats in Host x. One beat in seconds is where the live
// tempo enters, which is why this is derived per block rather than in @slider (a
// tempo change does not re-run @slider, so a value computed there would go
// stale).
// `>= 3`: a DURATION reads as beats in EITHER host mode. "N per beat" is a rate,
// not a length -- a start delay of "8 per beat" is meaningless -- so mode 4 takes
// the same beats-to-seconds factor mode 3 does. This said `== 3` and so silently
// treated a mode-4 instance's durations as raw seconds. (R21 sweep, 2026-09-06.)
tr_k            = time_mode >= 3 ? 60 / max(tempo, 0.001) : 1;
start_delay_sec = slider41 * tr_k;
// Play for and Rest for (targets 53, 54) while driven; the gate still switches on
// only when both SLIDERS are above zero, which is what this always tested.
pr_play_sec     = (mod_active[85] ? max(0.001, slider42 + au_key(85, 3, slider42, time_mode >= 3 ? 2 : 0)) : slider42) * tr_k;
pr_rest_sec     = (mod_active[86] ? max(0.001, slider43 + au_key(86, 3, slider43, time_mode >= 3 ? 2 : 0)) : slider43) * tr_k;
pr_enabled      = slider42 * tr_k > 0 && slider43 * tr_k > 0;
morph_at_rest   = slider44;   // 0 walk through, 1 freeze in place
out_at_rest     = slider45;   // 0 pass-through, 1 silence
// --- Drift period unit (slider51) and Ramp time unit (slider59). Each is one
//     factor: SECONDS PER UNIT, so a stored value times its factor is seconds.
//     Derived here, per block, and NOT in @slider, for the same reason auto_time
//     is: Beats reads the live tempo, and Cycles reads auto_time, and neither
//     re-runs @slider when it changes.
//
//     Cycles means one whole Auto-morph traversal, which is what auto_time is.
//     It is the one unit here that can be meaningless -- Auto-morph Off leaves
//     no cycle to count -- which is exactly why neither control DEFAULTS to it.
//     auto_time is floored at 0.01 s where it is derived, so neither factor can
//     reach zero and stall a period.
//
//     Switching a unit REINTERPRETS the number rather than converting it, which
//     is how the whole suite behaves: the user moved a visible control that
//     names the unit on itself, so nothing is silent about it.
drift_unit_sec  = slider51 == 0 ? auto_time :
                  slider51 == 1 ? 1 :
                                  60 / max(tempo, 0.001);
ramp_unit_sec   = slider59 == 0 ? auto_time :
                  slider59 == 1 ? 1 :
                  slider59 == 2 ? 60 :
                                  60 / max(tempo, 0.001);
// Drift period is a SECONDS value everywhere it is consumed. It used to follow
// Rate Mode; it follows its own unit control now.
drift_per_k     = drift_unit_sec;

// Wash grain (target 3), in ms, while driven. Rebuilding the grain is the cheap
// path (see below), so a drift on it is safe.
eff_grain_ms = mod_active[3] ? max(5, min(1000, grain_ms + au_key(3, 3, grain_ms, 3))) : grain_ms;

// Transport play-edge: immediate runtime setup -- rebuild window, clear the
// accumulator, reset runtime (captures themselves are NOT touched). With
// ext_noinit, @init no longer does this on play, so it's explicit here. The
// heavy spectrum/harmonic re-derive is NO LONGER done unconditionally here --
// it happens only when the analysis is actually stale, which is what stops the
// every-play crackle.
((play_state == 1 || play_state == 5) && (last_ps != 1 && last_ps != 5)) ? (
  build_synwin(floor(eff_grain_ms * srate / 1000));
  clear_accum();
  inpos = 0; rms_smooth = 0; morph_phase = 0;
  last_gsamp = floor(eff_grain_ms * srate / 1000); last_cappoint = cap_point01;
  last_capavg = cap_avg;
  // Snap the Overtone smoothers so a saved-on overtone does not chirp up the
  // series over 30 ms on every play.
  ot_h_smooth = ot_harm_base; ot_db_smooth = ot_depth_db; ot_w_smooth = ot_width;
  cap_dirty = 0;
  // (Drift reset is handled by its own transport block below — it must also
  //  catch loop-wraps / rewinds, which this capture-engine play-edge does not.)
  // Re-derive ONLY if the analysis is actually stale. In the normal
  // capture-then-play flow the spectra are already valid, so this does NOTHING
  // -- that is what kills the "crackle every time I hit play" (the old code
  // re-analyzed every slot on every play-edge, N heavy FFT+YIN passes crammed
  // into one block). When it IS stale -- a cold project load that hasn't been
  // scrubbed yet, including an offline render from cold -- derive in full here.
  // At the play-edge the accumulator was just cleared (no grains yet), so this
  // lump lands in the silent onset; and offline render has no real-time deadline
  // anyway, so it guarantees the render starts correct from sample zero.
  analysis_valid == 0 ? (
    si2 = 0; loop(n_used, compute_spectrum(si2); analyze_harm(si2); si2 += 1; );
    analysis_valid = 1;
  );
);
last_ps = play_state;

// --- Drift restart (Drift restart slider). "Restart on play" (default): drift
//     jumps to the start of its cycle the moment the transport begins playing
//     from a stop, so multiple tracks reset TOGETHER and their drifts stay in
//     sync (no clashy out-of-phase pitch motion). "Free-running": the transport
//     is ignored and drift evolves continuously — for a loop playing under/over
//     an unbroken drift, or any continuously-evolving texture. A loop repeat is
//     deliberately NOT a restart in either mode (drift flows straight across
//     loop boundaries); own edge state, independent of the capture play-edge. ---
drift_now_playing = (play_state == 1 || play_state == 5);
(drift_now_playing && drift_was_playing == 0) ? (
  // Ramp ALWAYS restarts its ride on transport play (its wind-down begins from
  // the current values each pass) — the suite's Speed-Ramp transport behaviour.
  ri = 0; loop(N_TARGETS, ramp_t_mem[ri] = 0; ramp_delay_elapsed_mem[ri] = 0; ri += 1; );
  // Drift restarts only in Restart-on-play mode (Free-running ignores transport).
  drift_restart_free == 0 ? (
    di = 0; loop(N_TARGETS,
      target_drift_phase[di]  = 0; target_drift_prev[di]  = 0;
      target_drift_curr[di]   = 0; target_drift_offset[di] = 0;
      di += 1;
    );
  );
);
drift_was_playing = drift_now_playing;

// Wash grain -> CHEAP: rebuild only the synthesis grain, immediately, every
// block if it's moving. No re-analysis, no clear -> safe to automate, no drops.
gsamp = floor(eff_grain_ms * srate / 1000);
gsamp != last_gsamp ? ( last_gsamp = gsamp; build_synwin(gsamp); );

// Capture point -> HEAVY (re-FFT + YIN). Debounced so a by-ear scrub re-derives
// once, when it settles, in one lump. (Fresh load is handled at the play-edge
// above -- analysis_valid starts 0 in @init.) NOT an automation target.
// A cold load analyses its captures at once, stopped or playing. A copy saved on
// Capture point 0 and Capture average 1 looked unchanged here and stayed SILENT until
// the first play (measured 2026-09-12: `breathing` #1). Rozaya: "on project load, to
// walk into silence? cmmon". The settle below derives every captured slot at its own
// point; a play edge first still does it there instead.
analysis_valid == 0 && n_used > 0 && !cap_dirty ? ( cap_dirty = 1; settle = 0; );
cap_point01 != last_cappoint ? ( last_cappoint = cap_point01; cap_dirty = 1; settle = 0; );
// Capture average is global, so a change to it invalidates EVERY captured slot,
// not just the focused one. Flag that separately: the settle below needs to know
// which SCOPE to re-derive, and the two causes no longer share one.
cap_avg != last_capavg ? ( last_capavg = cap_avg; cap_dirty = 1; capavg_dirty = 1; settle = 0; );
cap_dirty ? (
  settle += 1;
  // On settle, re-derive all captured slots in ONE pass (debounced, so a by-ear
  // scrub recomputes once when it stops moving). Deliberately a single lump, not
  // sliced across blocks: one brief glitch during the scrub beats several smaller
  // glitches smeared into the audio you're listening to. Marks the analysis fresh
  // so the play-edge above won't redo it.
  settle >= 4 ? (
    analysis_valid == 0 ? (
      // Cold project load scrubbed before the first play: nothing is derived
      // yet, so derive ALL captured slots -- each at its OWN stored point.
      si2 = 0; loop(n_used, compute_spectrum(si2); analyze_harm(si2); si2 += 1; );
      analysis_valid = 1;
    ) : capavg_dirty ? (
      // Warm, and the GLOBAL Capture average moved: it describes how every slot
      // is read, so every captured slot has to come back through the analysis.
      si2 = 0; loop(n_used, compute_spectrum(si2); analyze_harm(si2); si2 += 1; );
    ) : (
      // Warm: only the focused slot's capture point moved (that one IS per slot),
      // so re-derive JUST that slot -- scrubbing one slot no longer retunes the
      // others (the bug this fix addresses).
      // On All slots the point moved on all eight, so all eight come back.
      cap_all_scrub ? (
        si2 = 0; loop(n_used, compute_spectrum(si2); analyze_harm(si2); si2 += 1; );
      ) : (
        compute_spectrum(cap_slot); analyze_harm(cap_slot);
      );
    );
    cap_dirty = 0; capavg_dirty = 0; cap_all_scrub = 0;
  );
);

// On All slots one press grabs the same moment into all eight -- the same eight
// analyses in one block that a Capture average change already runs.
(cap_trig > 0.5 && cap_last <= 0.5) ? ( cap_all ? ( cpk = 0; loop(NSLOTS, capture(cpk); cpk += 1; ); ) : capture(cap_slot); slider2 = 0; cap_trig = 0; sliderchange(slider2); );  // momentary: auto-release Capture-now so it can never be SAVED on (which re-captured silence on every load)
cap_last = cap_trig;

// --- Everything DERIVED from a @serialize bank is computed here, every block,
//     and deliberately not in @slider.
//
// This is the bug that ate an evening. @slider and @serialize restore state by
// two independent paths with NO guaranteed relative order, and on a track
// duplicate @init may not run at all. Deriving these in @slider meant that
// whenever @slider ran first, they were computed from banks that were still at
// their defaults -- every layer gain 0, every ratio 1, every drift target
// inactive -- and then @serialize restored the banks underneath them and
// nothing ever recomputed. The BANK was perfect and the arrays the audio engine
// actually reads were empty, which is a horrible failure to diagnose: the
// values are all still there, the plugin is simply not using them.
//
// Diagnostic signature, and it is a good one: the settings come back the moment
// you touch ANY control, because that runs @slider. Transport play/stop does
// not fix it (it never calls @slider), which is the tell that separates this
// from an @init / ext_noinit problem. "Wiggling the slider brought them back"
// is this bug and almost nothing else.
//
// @block has no such ordering problem: it runs every block forever, so by the
// first sample of audio these are correct no matter which order anything else
// happened in. The cost is 11 pow() and a 19-iteration loop per block, which
// against a 32768-point FFT per grain does not register.
//
// RULE FOR THIS FILE: a value derived from a serialized bank belongs in @block.
// Only values derived from a SLIDER may live in @slider, because those arrive
// with the slider itself.
// Every layer's pitch, an offset from Transpose in its own units (2026-09-13). Layer 1 is
// the Original and moves like the rest. Pitch (16-31) and fine tune (33-48) are targets.
// At Semitones with no fine tune this is the number it always was.
lz = 0;
loop(N_LAY_SLOTS,
  layer_ratio[lz] = pow(2.0, (pv_semis(lay_punit[lz], lay_semi[lz] + (mod_active[LP_T0+lz] ? au_key(LP_T0+lz, 1, lay_semi[lz], lay_punit[lz]) : 0))
                             + pv_semis(lay_funit[lz], lay_fine[lz] + (mod_active[LF_T0+lz] ? au_key(LF_T0+lz, 1, lay_fine[lz], lay_funit[lz]) : 0)))/12);
  lz += 1;
);
// Inactive is a gain of 0 and nothing else -- the dB the user set stays in the
// bank untouched, so switching back restores it exactly. Everything downstream
// gates on this gain, so an inactive layer costs no CPU.
// Solo. Any layer soloed silences every layer that isn't, AND the base voice --
// solo means "let me hear this one thing", so leaving the un-transposed morph
// underneath would defeat the point. Solo deliberately OVERRIDES Inactive: you
// solo something in order to hear it, and a solo that returns silence because
// the layer was also muted is a bad thirty seconds for anyone working by ear.
// A level of -60 is still silent, because that is a level, not a mute.
// The original is in the scan and in the gain loop, so soloing it works the
// same way soloing a layer does -- and so does muting it, or turning it down
// under an octave. It gets no interval because it IS the interval everything
// else is measured from; its gain lands in layer_gain[LAY_ORIG] and the base
// voice and base wash both read it as base_gain below.
any_solo = 0;
lz = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, lay_solo[lz] >= 0.5 ? any_solo = 1; lz += 1; );
lz = 0;
loop(N_LAY_SLOTS,
  lay_audible = any_solo ? (lay_solo[lz] >= 0.5) : (lay_active[lz] >= 0.5);
  layer_gain[lz] = (lay_audible < 0.5 || lay_db_base[lz] <= -60) ? 0 : pow(10, lay_db_base[lz]/20);
  lz += 1;
);
base_gain = layer_gain[LAY_ORIG];
// td_active gates the drift-advance loop; mod_active gates the effective-value
// recompute (drift OR ramp configured). A target with neither stays exactly at
// its base value -- bit-identical to no modulation. Same restore hazard: drift
// configured in a saved project simply would not run until you touched a
// control, which has been true since the drift work and was never noticed
// because touching a control is what you do next anyway.
dvi = 0; loop(N_TARGETS,
  // Period 0 turns this target's drift OFF, matching the rest of the suite
  // (2026-09-08). Without it a 0 makes `max(dper * srate, 1)` advance a whole
  // drift cycle per SAMPLE -- the fastest possible wander, where the control
  // plainly reads as a disable.
  td_active[dvi]  = (target_drift_up[dvi] > 0 || target_drift_down[dvi] > 0) && target_drift_per[dvi] > 0 ? 1 : 0;
  mod_active[dvi] = (td_active[dvi] || ramp_by_mem[dvi] != 0) ? 1 : 0;
  dvi += 1;
);

@sample
inbuf[inpos] = (spl0 + spl1) * 0.5;
inpos += 1; inpos >= INRING ? inpos = 0;

// --- TRANSPORT: Start delay, then the Play/Rest gate ------------------------
//
// Both count WALL-CLOCK SECONDS here; @block has already turned a beat count
// into seconds when Rate Mode is Beats, so nothing below needs to know which
// unit the user typed in.
//
// The input ring above is filled BEFORE this, unconditionally and on purpose:
// the analysis window has to stay warm through a rest, or the first grain after
// one would be built from a buffer full of whatever was there when the rest
// began. Same reasoning as the suite's other rest gates -- a gate says "don't
// be heard", never "stop watching".
start_delay_elapsed < start_delay_sec ? (
  start_delay_elapsed += 1 / srate;
  in_rest = 1;
) : (
  pr_enabled ? (
    pr_accum += 1 / srate;
    pr_accum >= (pr_resting ? pr_rest_sec : pr_play_sec) ? (
      pr_accum = 0;
      pr_resting = 1 - pr_resting;
    );
    in_rest = pr_resting;
  ) : (
    in_rest = 0;
  );
);
// TWO different holds, with different reach. Getting this wrong once is why the
// distinction is spelled out.
//
// The START DELAY holds everything -- Drift, Ramp and the morph walk. That
// matches the sweeping filters, where the delay skips the whole engine: the
// delay means "the piece has not begun yet", and arriving mid-wander defeats it.
//
// A REST holds only the MORPH WALK, and only on Freeze in place. Drift and Ramp
// keep running through a rest, which is what every sibling does -- their "LFO at
// rest" freezes the LFO and nothing else.
in_start_delay = start_delay_elapsed < start_delay_sec;
mod_frozen     = in_start_delay;                                    // Drift, Ramp
morph_frozen   = in_start_delay || (in_rest && morph_at_rest == 1); // the walk

// --- Drift: advance each CONFIGURED target and compute its current offset.
//     Period is wall-clock SECONDS (this plugin has no musical clock). Shape
//     math (Sine / Triangle / Random value-noise) matches the rest of the
//     suite. Offset is asymmetric: positive half scales by `up`, negative by
//     `down`. Runs before every consumer below so grain/voice/output all agree. ---
mo_k = 0;
loop(N_TARGETS,
  di = mo_order[mo_k];   // the old twenty-four first, in their old order -- see @init
  td_active[di] ? (
    // The bank holds the slider's own unit, like every other per-target bank,
    // so convert to seconds here, where the live tempo is available and
    // its own unit control decides the factor: 1 for Seconds (which is what
    // every project in the library is on, so this stays a no-op for all of
    // them), one beat for Beats, one Auto-morph traversal for Cycles.
    dper = target_drift_per[di] * drift_per_k;
    // Play/Rest. Windows are whole drift PERIODS and pr_frozen is ticked every
    // sample whether frozen or not, so a FRACTION is meaningful and chooses
    // where the parameter parks: x.25 the crest, x.75 the trough, x.0 and x.5
    // neutral. A whole number parks at no-change every time, which is nearly
    // inaudible -- an awkward fraction rotates the park point instead.
    // While frozen the phase is held and the shape below is still computed from
    // it, so the offset holds where it stopped rather than snapping to zero.
    // Two independent reasons to hold: this target's own Drift play/rest, and
    // the plugin-wide transport (Start delay, or a Play/Rest rest with
    // the Start delay). pr_frozen is still TICKED either
    // way, so a transport rest does not also stall the drift's own gate -- it
    // holds the wander without desynchronising the thing that decides when the
    // wander holds.
    dfrozen = pr_frozen(drift_pr_accum, drift_pr_resting, di,
                        1.0 / max(dper * srate, 1),
                        target_drift_play[di], target_drift_rest[di]) || mod_frozen;
    dfrozen ? (
      dphase = target_drift_phase[di];
    ) : (
      dphase = target_drift_phase[di] + 1.0 / max(dper * srate, 1);
      dphase >= 1.0 ? (
        dphase -= 1.0;
        target_drift_shape[di] == 2 ? (
          target_drift_prev[di] = target_drift_curr[di];
          target_drift_curr[di] = rand(2) - 1;   // new random target in [-1,1]
        );
      );
      target_drift_phase[di] = dphase;
    );
    target_drift_shape[di] == 0 ? (
      dshape = sin(TWOPI * dphase);
    ) : target_drift_shape[di] == 1 ? (
      dshape = dphase < 0.25 ? (4*dphase) : dphase < 0.75 ? (2 - 4*dphase) : (4*dphase - 4);
    ) : (
      dshape = target_drift_prev[di] + (target_drift_curr[di] - target_drift_prev[di]) * dphase;
    );
    target_drift_offset[di] = dshape > 0 ? dshape * target_drift_up[di] : dshape * target_drift_down[di];
  );
  mo_k += 1;
);
// --- Ramp: while Engaged, each target with a duration advances its own ride
//     0..1 over that duration, after its own start delay; while Off, all rides
//     freeze and resume. The offset (ride * by) is computed every sample
//     (frozen when not engaged), and folds into the effective values alongside
//     the Drift offset below. ---
// mod_frozen holds every ride during the Start delay. A REST does not stop a
// ride -- that matches the siblings. Engage is unchanged and still the
// user's own freeze/resume switch; this is the transport's.
ramp_engaged && !mod_frozen ? (
  ri = 0;
  loop(N_TARGETS,
    ramp_dur_mem[ri] > 0 ? (
      rdelay_samps = ramp_delay_mem[ri] * ramp_unit_sec * srate;
      ramp_delay_elapsed_mem[ri] < rdelay_samps ? (
        ramp_delay_elapsed_mem[ri] += 1;
      ) : (
        // Play/Rest -- "ramp on one side, stairs on the other" (Rozaya,
        // 2026-09-04). Windows are whole RAMP TIME UNITS, matching Ramp
        // duration.
        // The rests come OUT of the duration rather than extending it: the
        // advancing step is scaled by (play+rest)/play, so a stepped ramp and
        // a smooth one of the same duration arrive on the destination
        // together. Freezing without the compensation would make them drift
        // steadily apart, which is the reading Rozaya explicitly did NOT hear
        // and is not the effect. Off unless BOTH windows are > 0.
        rpl = ramp_play_mem[ri];
        rrs = ramp_rest_mem[ri];
        rhold = pr_frozen(ramp_pr_accum, ramp_pr_resting, ri,
                          1.0 / max(ramp_unit_sec * srate, 1), rpl, rrs);
        // One-armed on purpose: an empty else-branch is a silent compile
        // failure in eel2 (CLAUDE.md).
        !rhold ? (
          rstep = 1.0 / max(srate * ramp_dur_mem[ri] * ramp_unit_sec, 1);
          (rpl > 0 && rrs > 0) ? rstep *= (rpl + rrs) / rpl;
          ramp_t_mem[ri] += rstep;
          ramp_t_mem[ri] > 1.0 ? ramp_t_mem[ri] = 1.0;
        );
      );
    );
    ri += 1;
  );
);
ri = 0; loop(N_TARGETS, ramp_offset_mem[ri] = ramp_t_mem[ri] * ramp_by_mem[ri]; ri += 1; );

// Effective (base + Drift wander + Ramp ride) values, clamped to each param's
// real range. A derived global (hlevel/wlevel, pitch_ratio, voice_gain,
// voice_detune) is only overwritten when its target is being modulated at all,
// so a plugin with no Drift and no Ramp is bit-identical to before.
// Target numbers below are the 2026-09-13 list: Texture 2, Spread 4, Transpose 5, Fine tune 6,
// Tuning reference 7, Stereo width 8, Low cut 10, High cut 11, Overtone harmonic 12, Output level 84.
mo0 = target_drift_offset[2] + ramp_offset_mem[2];
mod_active[2] ? (   // Texture: internal 0..1, offset is in 0-100 Texture units
  eff_tex = max(0, min(1, texture + mo0 * 0.01));
  hlevel = cos(eff_tex * PI * 0.5); wlevel = sin(eff_tex * PI * 0.5);
);
// Spread reaches its slider's 1000 Hz (2026-09-12). It stopped at 150, a limit the range
// sweep widened the slider past and left here. Rozaya: "That should have been lifted with
// the slider extention, so... go for it". No saved copy sat above 150 (123 read).
eff_spread_hz    = max(0,  min(1000, spread_hz    + (mod_active[4] ? au_key(4, 2, spread_hz, srate / FFTSIZE) : 0)));
// Tuning reference (7) first: it is what an Hz value counts from.
tuning_ref = mod_active[7] ? max(20, min(2000, tuning_base + au_key(7, 2, tuning_base, 20))) : tuning_base;
(mod_active[5] || mod_active[6] || mod_active[7]) ? (   // Transpose, Fine tune, Tuning reference
  // NOT clamped -- same reasoning as the twin in spectral_vowel_passage.
  // Pinning this to the slider's own +/-36 meant Drift and Ramp flattened
  // against the edge: the modulation kept moving and the sound stopped. The
  // spectral read is already bounds-guarded and the voice path only makes a
  // frequency, so an extreme value is inaudible rather than unsafe.
  // On Target default Transpose keeps the sum exactly as it was always written.
  eff_semi = pv_semis(pitch_unit, target_drift_unit[5] == 0 && ramp_by_unit[5] == 0 ? pitch_semi + target_drift_offset[5] + ramp_offset_mem[5]
                                                                                   : pitch_semi + au_key(5, 1, pitch_semi, pitch_unit))
           + pv_semis(pitch_funit, pitch_fine + au_key(6, 1, pitch_fine, pitch_funit));
  pitch_ratio = pow(2.0, eff_semi/12);
);
eff_stereo_width = max(0,  min(100, stereo_width + (mod_active[8] ? target_drift_offset[8] + ramp_offset_mem[8] : 0)));
mod_active[8] ? ( voice_detune = eff_stereo_width * 0.01 * VOICE_DETUNE_MAX; );
// Low cut reaches its slider's 20000 Hz (2026-09-13). It stopped at 500, the same leftover
// as Spread's 150: the hidden-limit audit heard 500 and 20000 render bit-identical.
eff_lowcut_hz    = max(0,  min(20000, lowcut_hz    + (mod_active[10] ? au_key(10, 2, lowcut_hz, 20) : 0)));
mod_active[84] ? (   // Output level (dB) -- named Voice level until 2026-08
  eff_vdb = max(-60, min(12, voice_db_base + target_drift_offset[84] + ramp_offset_mem[84]));
  voice_gain = eff_vdb <= -60 ? 0 : pow(10, eff_vdb/20);
);
// --- High cut (target 7). Derived into the four numbers both engines read:
//     hc_bound (stop entirely), hc_lo (start fading), hc_inv (fade slope) and
//     hc_top. OFF is not a special case downstream -- it is expressed as a fade
//     that starts at 1e9 Hz, so every partial multiplies by exactly 1.0 and the
//     Nyquist test compares against exactly srate*0.5, as it always did. The
//     fade (the top fifth of the cut) is there because this is a Drift/Ramp
//     target: a hard gate would step each partial off as the cut swept past it. ---
eff_hicut_hz = max(200, min(20000, hicut_hz + (mod_active[11] ? au_key(11, 2, hicut_hz, 20) : 0)));
hc_on = eff_hicut_hz < 19999.5 ? 1 : 0;
hc_on ? (
  hc_top = eff_hicut_hz; hc_lo = eff_hicut_hz * 0.8;
  hc_inv = 1 / max(1, hc_top - hc_lo);
  hc_bound = min(srate*0.5, hc_top);
) : (
  hc_top = srate*0.5; hc_lo = 1000000000; hc_inv = 0; hc_bound = srate*0.5;   // EEL2 has NO scientific notation -- 1e9 is a syntax error
);
// --- Layer levels (targets LAY_T0..LAY_T0+N_LAY_SLOTS-1, one per layer in ladder
//     order). Recomputed per sample only for a layer whose level is actually
//     being driven; otherwise @slider's value stands.
//     There is deliberately NO normalisation here. An earlier build held total
//     power steady as layers arrived, so the base gave a little back each time
//     one came in. That is defensible in the abstract and wrong in use: it
//     means the level you just set moves the level you set before it, so gain
//     staging becomes a chase against a soundscape the plugin is also editing.
//     The dB on the slider is the dB you get; levels add, and the meter is
//     where you find out. (The wash needed a matching fix -- see gen_grain.) ---
nlay_on = 0;
lz = 0;
loop(N_LAY_SLOTS,
  // Drift and Ramp move the level, never the switch: an Inactive layer stays
  // silent however far its level target wanders, so arming drift on a layer you
  // have muted cannot bring it back without you.
  (mod_active[LEV_T0+lz] && (any_solo ? lay_solo[lz] >= 0.5 : lay_active[lz] >= 0.5)) ? (
    eff_ldb = max(-60, min(0, lay_db_base[lz] + target_drift_offset[LEV_T0+lz] + ramp_offset_mem[LEV_T0+lz]));
    layer_gain[lz] = eff_ldb <= -60 ? 0 : pow(10, eff_ldb/20);
  );
  // nlay_on gates the pitch-shifted synthesis only, so the original never sets
  // it -- it is the base path, which always runs.
  (lz != LAY_ORIG && layer_gain[lz] > 0.0001) ? ( nlay_on = 1; );
  lz += 1;
);
base_gain = layer_gain[LAY_ORIG];

// morph_frozen, not mod_frozen -- the walk is what "Rest mode" governs.
// eff_morph01 is deliberately left holding its last value rather than
// recomputed, so the spectrum sits still instead of snapping to a slot edge.
automorph_mode > 0.5 && !morph_frozen ? (
  automorph_mode == 1 ? (
    // Sweep: triangle wander, morph runs 0 -> 1 -> 0 forever.
    morph_phase += 1 / (srate * auto_time);
    morph_phase >= 2 ? morph_phase -= 2;
    eff_morph01 = morph_phase <= 1 ? morph_phase : 2 - morph_phase;
  ) : automorph_mode == 2 ? (
    // Glide once: ramp 0 -> 1 and hold at the last slot.
    morph_phase += 1 / (srate * auto_time);
    morph_phase > 1 ? morph_phase = 1;
    eff_morph01 = morph_phase;
  ) : (
    // Drift = Sweep in shuffled order, crossfading DIRECTLY between consecutive
    // slots (never routing through the in-between ones). Advance the crossfade
    // cur_slot -> nxt_slot; when it completes, cur becomes nxt and we pull the
    // next slot from the shuffle bag (reshuffle once we've been through them all).
    // Each leg = auto_time/n_used, so one full pass = auto_time, each slot an
    // equal share. Direct A->B means every leg is the same single crossfade
    // regardless of which slots -> no whoosh, nothing in the middle to over-dwell.
    n_used < 2 ? ( cur_slot = 0; nxt_slot = 0; xfade = 0; ) : (
      // Lazily build the shuffle bag the first sample Drift actually runs with a
      // valid capture set -- NOT on mode entry, because on a reload that can fire
      // before the captures finish loading (n_used still 0), which stuck cur==nxt
      // on slot 0. Doing it here means n_used is always real.
      drift_primed != 1 ? (
        last_slot = -1; shuffle_bag(); bag_pos = 0;
        cur_slot = driftbag[bag_pos]; bag_pos += 1;
        nxt_slot = driftbag[bag_pos]; bag_pos += 1;
        xfade = 0; drift_primed = 1;
      );
      xfade += 1 / max(1, auto_time * srate / n_used);
      xfade >= 1 ? (
        cur_slot = nxt_slot; xfade = 0;
        bag_pos >= n_used ? ( last_slot = cur_slot; shuffle_bag(); bag_pos = 0; );   // through them all -> reshuffle
        nxt_slot = driftbag[bag_pos]; bag_pos += 1;
      );
    );
  );
) : (
  // Morph (target 0) moves the Morph slider's own position, so it acts where that
  // slider does: while Auto-morph is Off.
  eff_morph01 = mod_active[0] ? max(0, min(1, morph01 + mo_q(0) * 0.01)) : morph01;
);

// Shared slot selection -- BOTH engines read vsi/vsj/vmfr so they always agree.
audition_mode < 0.5 ? (
  // Focused-slot monitor: play exactly the capture slot, ignore morph.
  vsi = cap_slot; vsj = cap_slot; vmfr = 0;
) : automorph_mode == 3 ? (
  // Drift: DIRECT crossfade between the two consecutive shuffled slots (not a
  // position on the slot line), so it steps straight from one to the next.
  vsi = cur_slot; vsj = nxt_slot; vmfr = xfade;
) : (
  // Manual morph / Sweep / Glide: a position along the fixed slot line, blending
  // the two adjacent slots it sits between.
  vnu = n_used < 1 ? 1 : n_used;
  vmp = eff_morph01 * (vnu - 1);
  vsi = floor(vmp); vmfr = vmp - vsi;
  vsi >= vnu - 1 ? ( vsi = vnu - 1; vmfr = 0; );
  vsj = vsi < vnu - 1 ? vsi + 1 : vsi;
);

// --- Voice phase handover: hand the oscillator phases over with the slot. ---
// Bank A voices vsi, bank B voices vsj. When the pair steps on -- a Drift step
// between shuffled slots, or the morph position crossing a slot boundary -- the
// slot bank B had just crossfaded TO becomes bank A's, and A picked it up from
// its OWN phase accumulators: same magnitudes, same frequency, 64 partials all
// restarting mid-cycle, so the waveform STEPPED. That step is the click at slot
// changes. It reads as intermittent because its size is the difference between
// two unrelated phase sets -- sometimes inaudible, sometimes a pop -- which is
// what disguises a once-per-boundary bug as a random CPU dropout.
//
// Copying B's phases into A makes A resume the exact waveform B was mid-way
// through. B's own phases don't matter afterwards: it re-enters at zero level
// and fades up. A backwards step is the mirror case. The two are mutually
// exclusive because a two-slot bounce satisfies both tests at once, and running
// both copies would feed each bank the other's already-overwritten values.
// (Diagnosed and ear-tested in the sibling plugin, Passage, 2026-07-27.)
(vsi != last_vsi || vsj != last_vsj) ? (
  // SWAP the banks instead of copying them. The phase arrays are just base
  // addresses, so exchanging which one is "A" and which is "B" is four
  // assignments -- against the 2048 writes the copy version did in a single
  // sample, on every slot boundary. That was a spike on a fixed cycle, which is
  // exactly what "regular dropouts" sounds like once the steady load from a few
  // raised layers has eaten the headroom that used to absorb it.
  //
  // It is EXACTLY equivalent, and it also collapses the two cases the copy
  // version had to keep mutually exclusive:
  //   forward step  -- A must resume the waveform B was mid-way through.
  //                    Swap gives A = B's phases. B gets A's old ones, which do
  //                    not matter: B re-enters at zero level and fades up, so
  //                    any continuous phase will do.
  //   backward step -- the mirror, and the same swap serves it.
  // The copy version had to test which direction it was going and run one branch
  // or the other, because running both fed each bank the other's already
  // overwritten values. A swap has no such hazard -- there is nothing to
  // overwrite -- so the direction test goes away with it.
  //
  // (The click this all exists to prevent is unchanged: hand the phase over with
  // the slot, or every handoff steps the waveform. Diagnosed in Passage,
  // 2026-07-27; see the crossfade gotcha in CLAUDE.md.)
  swp = hph;       hph       = hphB;       hphB       = swp;
  swp = hphR;      hphR      = hphBR;      hphBR      = swp;
  swp = lay_hph;   lay_hph   = lay_hphB;   lay_hphB   = swp;
  swp = lay_hphR;  lay_hphR  = lay_hphBR;  lay_hphBR  = swp;
  last_vsi = vsi; last_vsj = vsj;
);

// --- Overtone: build the per-harmonic gain curve. ---
// Everything except a narrow window around the chosen harmonic is pushed DOWN, so
// one partial stands clear of its neighbours and reads as a pitch of its own.
// Attenuation rather than boost -- what a tract resonance physically does, and it
// means the control can only ever make the voice quieter.
//
// eff_ot_h is deliberately fractional: Drift and Ramp move it continuously, and a
// real singer's resonance sweeps rather than jumps, so at 7.5 the window sits
// across partials 7 and 8 and the emphasis hands over between them.
eff_ot_h = max(0, min(NHARM, ot_harm_base + target_drift_offset[12] + ramp_offset_mem[12]));
// Overtone lift (11) and width (12), while driven; otherwise @slider's values stand.
mod_active[13] ? ( ot_depth_db = max(0, min(48, ot_depth_base + mo_q(13))); );
mod_active[14] ? ( ot_width = max(0.5, min(4, ot_width_base + mo_q(14))); );
// Each layer's overtone harmonic (35-50). A drift moves only a layer that has its
// own harmonic; a follower (-1) stays a follower. A change rebuilds the curve.
lot_changed = 0; lok = 0;
loop(N_LAY_SLOTS,
  // Reads the BANK. A bulk rename of lay_ot_harm[lok] once turned this into
  // lay_ot_eff[lok], so it read its own output, stayed at -2, and every layer
  // followed the global harmonic -- caught by a pitched-voice test, 2026-09-11.
  lot_e = lay_ot_harm[lok];
  (lot_e >= 0 && mod_active[LOT_T0+lok]) ? ( lot_e = max(0, min(NHARM, lot_e + mo_q(LOT_T0+lok))); );
  lot_e != lay_ot_eff[lok] ? ( lay_ot_eff[lok] = lot_e; lot_changed = 1; );
  lok += 1;
);
abs(eff_ot_h - ot_h_smooth)     < 0.0005 ? ot_h_smooth  = eff_ot_h    : ot_h_smooth  += (eff_ot_h    - ot_h_smooth)  * ot_coeff;
abs(ot_depth_db - ot_db_smooth) < 0.0005 ? ot_db_smooth = ot_depth_db : ot_db_smooth += (ot_depth_db - ot_db_smooth) * ot_coeff;
abs(ot_width - ot_w_smooth)     < 0.0005 ? ot_w_smooth  = ot_width    : ot_w_smooth  += (ot_width    - ot_w_smooth)  * ot_coeff;
ot_on = (ot_h_smooth >= 0.5 && ot_db_smooth > 0.01) ? 1 : 0;
(ot_on != ot_on_last || (ot_on && (ot_h_smooth != ot_h_last || ot_db_smooth != ot_db_last || ot_w_smooth != ot_w_last || lot_changed))) ? (
  ot_on ? (
    // --- A resonance is a PEAK: the chosen partial is LIFTED, the others are left
    //     alone. This first shipped inverted -- floored everything and returned the
    //     chosen one to 1.0, so the curve's maximum gain was 1 and nothing was ever
    //     lifted. Normalising to the PEAK is arithmetically the same as ducking the
    //     whole voice, and it is audible as one (Rozaya, 2026-07-28). Ear-safety is
    //     kept by normalising to POWER instead: lift the partial, then scale the
    //     bank so summed RMS is unchanged. It still can never get louder; it just
    //     stops sounding like the voice ducking. Full writeup in Passage, which
    //     carries the same engine. ---
    otg  = pow(10, ot_db_smooth/20);           // how hard the resonance lifts it
    otw  = max(0.5, ot_w_smooth);
    oi = 0; loop(NHARM, ot_gain[oi] = 1; oi += 1; );   // everything else stays put
    olo = max(1, ceil(ot_h_smooth - otw));     // harmonic NUMBERS (index = number - 1)
    ohi = min(NHARM, floor(ot_h_smooth + otw));
    oi = olo;
    loop(max(0, ohi - olo + 1),
      od = abs(oi - ot_h_smooth) / otw;
      ow = od >= 1 ? 0 : (0.5 + 0.5*wtsin(od*PI + PI*0.5));
      ot_gain[oi-1] = 1 + (otg - 1)*ow;
      oi += 1;
    );
    ot_gain[0] = 1;   // the fundamental is never lifted -- it is the drone
    opw = 0; oi = 0; loop(NHARM, opw += ot_gain[oi]*ot_gain[oi]; oi += 1; );
    ot_norm = opw > 0 ? sqrt(NHARM / opw) : 1;
    oi = 0; loop(NHARM, ot_gain[oi] = ot_gain[oi] * ot_norm; oi += 1; );
    ot_boost = otg;                            // the wash rebuilds the window itself
    // Per-layer harmonics. Same curve and the same power normalisation, but
    // centred on that layer's own harmonic. Lift and width stay global -- they
    // are character, and character can reasonably be uniform across a stack;
    // the HARMONIC is the melodic choice, which is why it is the half that
    // goes per layer. Runs only for layers that actually override, so the
    // usual cost of this loop is zero.
    lok = 0;
    loop(N_LAY_SLOTS,
      lay_ot_eff[lok] >= 0 ? (
        lobo = lok*NHARM;
        oi = 0; loop(NHARM, lay_ot_gain[lobo+oi] = 1; oi += 1; );
        lay_ot_eff[lok] > 0 ? (
          olo = max(1, ceil(lay_ot_eff[lok] - otw));
          ohi = min(NHARM, floor(lay_ot_eff[lok] + otw));
          oi = olo;
          loop(max(0, ohi - olo + 1),
            od = abs(oi - lay_ot_eff[lok]) / otw;
            ow = od >= 1 ? 0 : (0.5 + 0.5*wtsin(od*PI + PI*0.5));
            lay_ot_gain[lobo+oi-1] = 1 + (otg - 1)*ow;
            oi += 1;
          );
          lay_ot_gain[lobo] = 1;   // the fundamental is never lifted here either
        );
        opw = 0; oi = 0; loop(NHARM, opw += lay_ot_gain[lobo+oi]*lay_ot_gain[lobo+oi]; oi += 1; );
        lonm = opw > 0 ? sqrt(NHARM / opw) : 1;
        oi = 0; loop(NHARM, lay_ot_gain[lobo+oi] = lay_ot_gain[lobo+oi] * lonm; oi += 1; );
        lay_ot_norm[lok] = lonm;      // the wash applies this itself, per layer
      ) : (
        lay_ot_norm[lok] = ot_norm;   // follower: the global curve, so the global norm
      );
      lok += 1;
    );
  ) : (
    oi = 0; loop(NHARM, ot_gain[oi] = 1; oi += 1; );
    ot_boost = 1; ot_norm = 1;
    lok = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, lay_ot_norm[lok] = 1; lok += 1; );
  );
  ot_on_last = ot_on; ot_h_last = ot_h_smooth; ot_db_last = ot_db_smooth; ot_w_last = ot_w_smooth;
);
// Wash-side position, in bins, off the morph-blended fundamental. Width is in
// harmonics for both engines, so here it becomes width x f0 in Hz.
//
// SOURCE bins, not output bins. `curmag` is the captured spectrum, and every
// layer -- the Original included -- reads it at its own scale (`i / lpr[lq]`)
// inside build_spectrum. Putting the window in source bins is what lets each
// layer pick its own harmonic of the SAME spectrum, and it is also the only
// domain in which the arithmetic is right: this used to fold `pitch_ratio` in
// and then index `curmag` with the result, which is an output-bin position
// applied to a source-bin array. See the note in gen_grain.
(ot_on && have > 0) ? (
  ot_f0src = (1-vmfr)*slot_f0[vsi] + vmfr*slot_f0[vsj];   // SOURCE fundamental, un-shifted
  ot_bpf   = FFTSIZE / srate;
  ot_binw  = max(1, ot_f0src * max(0.5, ot_w_smooth) * ot_bpf);
  ot_f0bin = ot_f0src * 1.5 * ot_bpf;
  ot_bin   = ot_f0src * ot_h_smooth * ot_bpf;   // the global position, for followers
  // ...and one centre per layer, so a layer running its own harmonic lifts a
  // different partial of the same captured spectrum.
  lok = 0;
  loop(N_LAY_SLOTS,
    lot_bin[lok] = ot_f0src * (lay_ot_eff[lok] >= 0 ? lay_ot_eff[lok] : ot_h_smooth) * ot_bpf;
    lok += 1;
  );
) : ( ot_bin = 0; );

// Denoise (7, percent) and Input level (51, dB), per sample only while driven.
mod_active[9] ? ( denoise_amt = max(0, min(100, denoise_base + mo_q(9))); );
mod_active[83] ? (
  mo_dry_db = max(-60, min(12, dry_db_base + mo_q(83)));
  dry_gain = mo_dry_db <= -60 ? 0 : pow(10, mo_dry_db/20);
);
dryL = spl0 * dry_gain; dryR = spl1 * dry_gain;

// --- wash (spectral) engine: read overlap-add accumulator, refill per hop ---
wL = accL[accpos]; accL[accpos] = 0;
wR = accR[accpos]; accR[accpos] = 0;
accpos += 1; accpos >= ACCLEN ? accpos = 0;
hopcount += 1;
// Skip grain synthesis (gen_grain is HEAVY: two FFTSIZE iFFTs) when the wash is
// inaudible -- the mirror of the voice engine's "skip when Texture is full wash"
// guard below. At Texture 0 (pure voice) wlevel is 0, so wL*wlevel contributes
// nothing anyway; building grains for it was pure wasted CPU -- a real per-grain
// FFT load that could tip a busy project into occasional real-time dropouts
// (the "crackle on every slot, non-clipping" symptom). The accumulator still
// drains via the read-and-clear above, so raising Texture off 0 fades the wash
// back in cleanly over ~one grain. Output is bit-identical while muted.
hopcount >= HOP ? ( hopcount = 0; (have > 0 && wlevel > 0.0001) ? gen_grain(); );

// --- harmonic (voice) engine: TWO morph voices (vsi/vsj), each at its OWN
// captured pitch, crossfaded by morph. Now STEREO: when Stereo width (->
// voice_detune) > 0 the right channel runs a slightly-detuned copy, so L/R beat
// slowly -> real width + a shimmer that softens the robotic edge of pure
// additive harmonics. At width 0 it's the exact original mono path. Skipped
// entirely when the voice is inaudible (Texture at full wash, hlevel ~ 0) --
// that reclaims the whole per-sample harmonic loop when you live on the wash.
(hlevel > 0.0001 && have > 0) ? (
  fA = slot_f0[vsi] * pitch_ratio; normA = slot_hnorm[vsi]; normA <= 0 ? normA = 1;
  fB = slot_f0[vsj] * pitch_ratio; normB = slot_hnorm[vsj]; normB <= 0 ? normB = 1;
  // The base voice is Layer 1 (2026-09-13), so it takes Layer 1's pitch; the other layers
  // stay offsets from fA and fB, from Transpose alone.
  fAo = fA * layer_ratio[LAY_ORIG]; fBo = fB * layer_ratio[LAY_ORIG];
  bA = vsi*NHARM; bB = vsj*NHARM;
  // Partial magnitudes depend only on the slot pair and the Overtone curve, so
  // they are the same for the base voice and for every layer. Computed ONCE per
  // sample instead of re-divided inside each layer's inner loop -- with several
  // layers up that was hundreds of redundant divisions per sample, on the path
  // that is already the expensive one at the voice end of Texture.
  // ...and while we are walking them, find the highest partial that actually
  // carries anything. Captured voices put nearly all their energy in the low
  // partials; the tail above some index sits at -70 dB and below relative to the
  // peak, contributes nothing audible, and costs exactly as much to synthesise
  // as the fundamental does -- times every layer, times four banks, every
  // sample. Stopping the synthesis loops at nharm_used is where the headroom
  // for a four-layer stack comes from.
  //
  // Contiguous by construction: this is the highest index above the threshold,
  // not a per-partial skip, so nothing is punched out of the middle of the
  // series. The dropped partials also stop advancing their phase, exactly as
  // the ones above Nyquist and above the High cut already do -- an inaudible
  // partial resuming from a stale phase is inaudible too.
  // The divisions happen ONCE, into hm_raw*, and everything downstream is a
  // multiply against them -- the base voice's Overtone curve here, and each
  // overriding layer's curve below.
  vn = 0; nharm_used = 1;
  loop(NHARM,
    hm_rawA[vn] = slot_harm[bA+vn]/normA;
    hm_rawB[vn] = slot_harm[bB+vn]/normB;
    hmA_arr[vn] = hm_rawA[vn] * ot_gain[vn];
    hmB_arr[vn] = hm_rawB[vn] * ot_gain[vn];
    (hmA_arr[vn] > HM_EPS || hmB_arr[vn] > HM_EPS) ? nharm_used = vn + 1;
    vn += 1;
  );
  // The same magnitudes again for any layer running its OWN Overtone harmonic.
  // This runs PER SAMPLE -- it is inside @sample, like the loop above it. An
  // earlier comment here claimed it ran per frame and cost nothing; that was
  // wrong, and it was copied out of a design note about the wash. What it
  // actually costs is two multiplies per harmonic per OVERRIDING layer, which
  // is small beside that layer's own synthesis loop (four phase banks) and is
  // exactly zero for a layer at the default -1, which is every layer until you
  // change one. Only audible layers are built.
  lok = 0;
  loop(N_LAY_SLOTS,
    (lay_ot_eff[lok] >= 0 && layer_gain[lok] > 0.0001) ? (
      lobo = lok*NHARM; vn = 0;
      loop(nharm_used,
        lay_hmA[lobo+vn] = hm_rawA[vn] * lay_ot_gain[lobo+vn];
        lay_hmB[lobo+vn] = hm_rawB[vn] * lay_ot_gain[lobo+vn];
        vn += 1;
      );
    );
    lok += 1;
  );
  voice_detune > 0 ? (
    // Stereo: left slightly flat, right slightly sharp (symmetric, so the
    // perceived pitch centre is unchanged). Separate R phase banks (hphR/hphBR)
    // run at the detuned frequency and drift against the L banks = width.
    fAL = fAo*(1-voice_detune); fAR = fAo*(1+voice_detune);
    fBL = fBo*(1-voice_detune); fBR = fBo*(1+voice_detune);
    vvAL = 0; vvAR = 0; vvBL = 0; vvBR = 0; vn = 0;
    loop(nharm_used,
      hmA = hmA_arr[vn];   // hoisted above; Overtone is already folded in
      // High cut: hcg fades a partial out across the top fifth of the cut, and
      // hc_bound stops computing it entirely. With High cut off hc_lo is huge and
      // hc_bound is srate*0.5, so hcg is exactly 1 and the test is the original
      // one -- this path stays bit-identical for a project that never sets it.
      // Phase advances INSIDE the audibility test. The wrap below subtracts
      // TWOPI at most once per sample, which only keeps up while the step is
      // smaller than TWOPI -- i.e. while the partial sits under the sample rate.
      // Above that the accumulator climbed without limit, and wtsin indexes its
      // table straight off it, so a runaway phase read clean past the end of the
      // sine table into whatever memory follows. Nothing above Nyquist is heard
      // anyway, so not advancing what isn't read makes every step that happens
      // smaller than PI by construction (and does slightly less work). Needs
      // f0 x pitch above ~750 Hz to reach, but Pitch is a Drift/Ramp target.
      lypf = (vn+1)*fAL; hcg = lypf > hc_lo ? (hc_top - lypf)*hc_inv : 1;
      lypf < hc_bound ? ( vvAL += hmA*hcg*wtsin(hph[vn]);
        hph[vn]   += TWOPI*(vn+1)*fAL/srate; hph[vn]   >= TWOPI ? hph[vn]   -= TWOPI; );
      lypf = (vn+1)*fAR; hcg = lypf > hc_lo ? (hc_top - lypf)*hc_inv : 1;
      lypf < hc_bound ? ( vvAR += hmA*hcg*wtsin(hphR[vn]);
        hphR[vn]  += TWOPI*(vn+1)*fAR/srate; hphR[vn]  >= TWOPI ? hphR[vn]  -= TWOPI; );
      hmB = hmB_arr[vn];
      lypf = (vn+1)*fBL; hcg = lypf > hc_lo ? (hc_top - lypf)*hc_inv : 1;
      lypf < hc_bound ? ( vvBL += hmB*hcg*wtsin(hphB[vn]);
        hphB[vn]  += TWOPI*(vn+1)*fBL/srate; hphB[vn]  >= TWOPI ? hphB[vn]  -= TWOPI; );
      lypf = (vn+1)*fBR; hcg = lypf > hc_lo ? (hc_top - lypf)*hc_inv : 1;
      lypf < hc_bound ? ( vvBR += hmB*hcg*wtsin(hphBR[vn]);
        hphBR[vn] += TWOPI*(vn+1)*fBR/srate; hphBR[vn] >= TWOPI ? hphBR[vn] -= TWOPI; );
      vn += 1;
    );
    hVL = base_gain * ((1-vmfr)*vvAL + vmfr*vvBL) * 0.08;   // ~match the wash's ~0.1 RMS
    hVR = base_gain * ((1-vmfr)*vvAR + vmfr*vvBR) * 0.08;
  ) : (
    // Mono (Stereo width 0): the exact original single-bank path, L = R.
    vvA = 0; vvB = 0; vn = 0;
    loop(nharm_used,
      // Phase advance moved inside the audibility guard -- see the stereo path.
      lypf = (vn+1)*fAo; hcg = lypf > hc_lo ? (hc_top - lypf)*hc_inv : 1;
      lypf < hc_bound ? ( vvA += hmA_arr[vn]*hcg*wtsin(hph[vn]);
        hph[vn]  += TWOPI*(vn+1)*fAo/srate; hph[vn]  >= TWOPI ? hph[vn]  -= TWOPI; );
      lypf = (vn+1)*fBo; hcg = lypf > hc_lo ? (hc_top - lypf)*hc_inv : 1;
      lypf < hc_bound ? ( vvB += hmB_arr[vn]*hcg*wtsin(hphB[vn]);
        hphB[vn] += TWOPI*(vn+1)*fBo/srate; hphB[vn] >= TWOPI ? hphB[vn] -= TWOPI; );
      vn += 1;
    );
    hVL = base_gain * ((1-vmfr)*vvA + vmfr*vvB) * 0.08;
    hVR = hVL;
  );
  // --- Layers: the same two morph voices again, at each layer's frequency
  // ratio, summed into the same output. BOTH voices, so a layer crossfades
  // between slots exactly as the base does and stays locked to the morph
  // position -- that lock is what makes an octave stack consonant where two
  // instances on Shuffle clash. Gated on the layer's own level, so a layer at
  // -60 dB costs nothing at all: skip the DSP, not merely its output (the
  // muted-layer rule behind the v2.18 crackle fix). Each layer keeps its own
  // phase banks; sharing the base's would put them at the wrong phase for both.
  // Upward layers get CHEAPER as they rise -- their partials cross Nyquist (or
  // the High cut) and stop being computed at all. ---
  nlay_on ? (
    lyk = 0;
    loop(N_LAY_SLOTS,
      lyg = layer_gain[lyk];
      // LAY_ORIG is the original, synthesised by the base path above.
      (lyk != LAY_ORIG && lyg > 0.0001) ? (
        lybo = lyk*NHARM;
        lyfa = fA*layer_ratio[lyk]; lyfb = fB*layer_ratio[lyk];
        // Layer harmonics (slider37, per layer, 0 = uncapped): a plain count,
        // never above what the source actually has (nharm_used). A layer with
        // no cap set costs exactly what it always cost -- this is opt-in only.
        lynh = lay_nharm[lyk] > 0 ? min(nharm_used, lay_nharm[lyk]) : nharm_used;
        // Which magnitude bank this layer sounds from: its own if it overrides
        // the Overtone harmonic, the shared one otherwise. A memory index is
        // just a number in EEL2, so this is one select per layer per sample.
        lyhA = lay_ot_eff[lyk] >= 0 ? (lay_hmA + lybo) : hmA_arr;
        lyhB = lay_ot_eff[lyk] >= 0 ? (lay_hmB + lybo) : hmB_arr;
        voice_detune > 0 ? (
          lyfal = lyfa*(1-voice_detune); lyfar = lyfa*(1+voice_detune);
          lyfbl = lyfb*(1-voice_detune); lyfbr = lyfb*(1+voice_detune);
          lyv_al = 0; lyv_ar = 0; lyv_bl = 0; lyv_br = 0; vn = 0;
          loop(lynh,
            hmA = lyhA[vn]; hmB = lyhB[vn];
            lypf = (vn+1)*lyfal; hcg = lypf > hc_lo ? (hc_top - lypf)*hc_inv : 1;
            lypf < hc_bound ? ( lyv_al += hmA*hcg*wtsin(lay_hph[lybo+vn]);
              lay_hph[lybo+vn]   += TWOPI*(vn+1)*lyfal/srate; lay_hph[lybo+vn]   >= TWOPI ? lay_hph[lybo+vn]   -= TWOPI; );
            lypf = (vn+1)*lyfar; hcg = lypf > hc_lo ? (hc_top - lypf)*hc_inv : 1;
            lypf < hc_bound ? ( lyv_ar += hmA*hcg*wtsin(lay_hphR[lybo+vn]);
              lay_hphR[lybo+vn]  += TWOPI*(vn+1)*lyfar/srate; lay_hphR[lybo+vn]  >= TWOPI ? lay_hphR[lybo+vn]  -= TWOPI; );
            lypf = (vn+1)*lyfbl; hcg = lypf > hc_lo ? (hc_top - lypf)*hc_inv : 1;
            lypf < hc_bound ? ( lyv_bl += hmB*hcg*wtsin(lay_hphB[lybo+vn]);
              lay_hphB[lybo+vn]  += TWOPI*(vn+1)*lyfbl/srate; lay_hphB[lybo+vn]  >= TWOPI ? lay_hphB[lybo+vn]  -= TWOPI; );
            lypf = (vn+1)*lyfbr; hcg = lypf > hc_lo ? (hc_top - lypf)*hc_inv : 1;
            lypf < hc_bound ? ( lyv_br += hmB*hcg*wtsin(lay_hphBR[lybo+vn]);
              lay_hphBR[lybo+vn] += TWOPI*(vn+1)*lyfbr/srate; lay_hphBR[lybo+vn] >= TWOPI ? lay_hphBR[lybo+vn] -= TWOPI; );
            vn += 1;
          );
          hVL += lyg * ((1-vmfr)*lyv_al + vmfr*lyv_bl) * 0.08;
          hVR += lyg * ((1-vmfr)*lyv_ar + vmfr*lyv_br) * 0.08;
        ) : (
          // Mono (Stereo width 0): one bank, L = R, mirroring the base voice.
          lyv_al = 0; lyv_bl = 0; vn = 0;
          loop(lynh,
            lypf = (vn+1)*lyfa; hcg = lypf > hc_lo ? (hc_top - lypf)*hc_inv : 1;
            lypf < hc_bound ? ( lyv_al += lyhA[vn]*hcg*wtsin(lay_hph[lybo+vn]);
              lay_hph[lybo+vn]  += TWOPI*(vn+1)*lyfa/srate; lay_hph[lybo+vn]  >= TWOPI ? lay_hph[lybo+vn]  -= TWOPI; );
            lypf = (vn+1)*lyfb; hcg = lypf > hc_lo ? (hc_top - lypf)*hc_inv : 1;
            lypf < hc_bound ? ( lyv_bl += lyhB[vn]*hcg*wtsin(lay_hphB[lybo+vn]);
              lay_hphB[lybo+vn] += TWOPI*(vn+1)*lyfb/srate; lay_hphB[lybo+vn] >= TWOPI ? lay_hphB[lybo+vn] -= TWOPI; );
            vn += 1;
          );
          lymix = lyg * ((1-vmfr)*lyv_al + vmfr*lyv_bl) * 0.08;
          hVL += lymix; hVR += lymix;
        );
      );
      lyk += 1;
    );
  );
) : ( hVL = 0; hVR = 0; );

// --- crossfade voice <-> wash (equal-power), gate on having a capture ---
have > 0 ? (
  voutL = hlevel*hVL + wlevel*wL;
  voutR = hlevel*hVR + wlevel*wR;
) : ( voutL = 0; voutR = 0; );
voutL *= voice_gain; voutR *= voice_gain;

// Rest gating. Pass-through (the default) mutes only what this plugin ADDS and
// leaves the dry input alone, so a rest is a hole in the texture rather than a
// hole in the track -- the right default for an effect sitting on someone's
// audio. Silence mutes everything, dry included.
//
// Applied to the SUM rather than inside the engines: the grain accumulator and
// the voice oscillators keep running and draining, so the plugin fades back in
// over about one grain when the rest ends instead of snapping. Muting inside
// would leave a stale accumulator to dump on the way back.
in_rest ? (
  out_at_rest == 1 ? (
    spl0 = 0;
    spl1 = 0;
  ) : (
    spl0 = dryL;
    spl1 = dryR;
  );
) : (
  spl0 = dryL + voutL;
  spl1 = dryR + voutR;
);

@serialize
// Persist captures across project save/reopen. Save only the RAW audio (source
// of truth) + counts; wash spectrum and harmonic analysis re-derive from slotraw
// on the first @block after load. Magic leads the stream so an old/absent blob
// degrades to "no captures" instead of scrambling state (CLAUDE.md gotcha).
// Magic:
//   7700001 -- 6 Drift/Ramp targets.
//   7700002 -- Overtone harmonic joined the target lists, making it 7.
//              The TARGET COUNT IS PART OF THE FORMAT -- the banks are written
//              back to back, so reading 7 where 6 were written walks the cursor
//              off the end of one and into the next. Hence a new magic rather
//              than an append. A 7700001 blob reads 6 and the seventh target
//              keeps its defaults.
//   7700004 -- current. High cut and the eleven layer levels joined the target
//              lists, making it 19. Same reasoning, same shape: a 7700002 blob
//              reads its 7 and the twelve new targets keep their defaults (no
//              drift, no ramp), which is exactly what those projects sounded
//              like. Targets were APPENDED, so every saved selector index still
//              points at the parameter it was pointing at. (7700003 was an
//              in-development layout with three layers and no octave stack; it
//              never left this branch, so nothing reads it.)
//   7700011 -- current. NO format change either, and no new bank: the two new
//              controls (Drift period unit at 39, Ramp time unit at 46) are
//              plain global sliders, not per-target values. The bump exists
//              solely so the blob is an exact witness of WHICH SLIDER LAYOUT
//              an instance was saved on -- the eleven controls above them each
//              moved up. A 7700010 blob means an instance still holds the
//              previous order and needs
//              tools/morpher_migrate_driftramp_units.py.
//   7700010 -- NO format change either; same reasoning as 7700009,
//              one layout later. The transport block (Start delay, Play/Rest,
//              Modulation at rest, Output at rest) went in at 30-34, pushing
//              Drift and Ramp up by five. A 7700009 blob is therefore an exact
//              witness that an instance still holds the PREVIOUS slider order
//              and needs tools/morpher_migrate_transport.py.
//   7700009 -- NO format change: the stream is byte-for-byte what
//              7700008 wrote, and 7700008 is read exactly the same way. The
//              bump exists solely so the blob is an EXACT gate for WHICH SLIDER
//              LAYOUT an instance was saved on -- Drift play/rest and Ramp
//              play/rest went into their logical positions at 35/36 and 41/42,
//              which pushed Drift restart and the whole Ramp group up. A
//              renumber cannot touch the blob, so a 7700008 blob is an
//              independent witness that an instance still holds the OLD slider
//              order and needs tools/morpher_migrate_playrest.py. That witness
//              is the only thing that made the Melody slider-insert repairable
//              without guessing (CLAUDE.md), so it is bumped here in the same
//              commit as the layout change, which is the rule.
//   7700008 -- The list became a PITCH LADDER: the original sits at
//              unison, mid-list, rather than at either end. Blobs permute
//              themselves; the SLIDER LINE needs the migration script.
//   7700007 -- The original moved to the FRONT of the Layer selector
//              and of the Drift/Ramp target list, where it belongs by meaning.
//              Blobs migrate themselves (a rotate, below); the SLIDER LINE does
//              not and needs tools/morpher_migrate_layer_order.py.
//   7700006 -- The ORIGINAL gained a bank slot of its own (16 config
//              entries, 24 targets) so it can be muted, levelled and SOLOED
//              like any layer. A 7700005 blob is read at 15 and the original
//              seeded to unity -- which is exactly how it sounded, since the
//              base voice had no level of its own to have saved.
//   7700005 -- The named non-octave intervals moved onto the Layer
//              selector (15 layers, 23 targets), the interval picker became
//              Solo, and a solo flag per layer joined the stream. 7700004 is
//              still READ and migrated rather than dropped -- it existed for one
//              evening on one machine, but layers had been dialled in against
//              it, and resetting someone's work to save fifteen lines is the
//              same disrespect as moving their values silently.
//   7700055 -- (2026-09-11). Fifty-five targets in control order. Every
//              older magic reads at its own width, runs the migrations below, and
//              is then remapped from the 24-target list, selectors included.
//   7700087 -- current (2026-09-13, the pitch layout). 87 targets, Layer 1-16 in their new
//              order, and APPENDED at the very end: every layer's pitch, pitch unit, fine
//              tune and fine tune unit, the Layer selector as shown (All included), and the
//              Drift amount unit and Ramp by unit per target. Read only from a blob that
//              WROTE them; an older one keeps @init's defaults (the ladder's intervals,
//              Semitones, no fine tune, Target default), which are what the slider
//              migration seeds, and walks 55 -> 87 and the ladder -> Layer 1-16 below.
ser_magic = 7700087;
file_var(0, ser_magic);
(ser_magic == 7700087 || ser_magic == 7700055 || ser_magic == 7700011 || ser_magic == 7700010 || ser_magic == 7700009 || ser_magic == 7700008 || ser_magic == 7700007 || ser_magic == 7700006 || ser_magic == 7700005 || ser_magic == 7700004 || ser_magic == 7700002 || ser_magic == 7700001) ? (
  ser_new = ser_magic >= 7700087;   // holds the pitch layout's appended banks
  n_ser_targets = ser_magic == 7700087 ? N_TARGETS : ser_magic == 7700055 ? 55 : ser_magic >= 7700006 ? 24 : ser_magic == 7700005 ? 23 :
                  ser_magic == 7700004 ? 19 : ser_magic == 7700002 ? 7 : 6;
  file_var(0, have);
  file_var(0, n_used);
  // Save/restore only the slots actually captured (n_used * MAXFFT), not the
  // full NSLOTS store -- so raising the slot count doesn't bloat the project
  // file with empty slots, and it scales with what's really in use. Backward
  // compatible: an old save that stored the full 4-slot block still restores
  // its captures (file_mem reads only n_used*MAXFFT, the leading slots, and
  // stops -- the trailing bytes were always empty tail slots anyway).
  file_mem(0, slotraw, n_used*MAXFFT);

  // --- Drift config, APPENDED at the end (safe without a magic bump). A
  //     pre-Drift project has the same 7700001 magic but no drift bytes; these
  //     reads run past EOF, and REAPER leaves the destination unchanged on a
  //     short read -- so old projects keep their captures and get default drift.
  //     Inserting mid-stream would misalign; appending is EOF-safe. ---
  file_mem(0, target_drift_up, n_ser_targets);
  file_mem(0, target_drift_down, n_ser_targets);
  file_mem(0, target_drift_per, n_ser_targets);
  file_mem(0, target_drift_shape, n_ser_targets);
  file_var(0, last_target_select);

  // --- Ramp config, appended after Drift. EOF-safe: a pre-Ramp project (drift
  //     only, or captures only) reads past EOF here and keeps default ramp. ---
  file_mem(0, ramp_by_mem, n_ser_targets);
  file_mem(0, ramp_dur_mem, n_ser_targets);
  file_mem(0, ramp_delay_mem, n_ser_targets);
  file_var(0, last_ramp_target);

  // --- Per-slot Capture point, appended after Ramp (EOF-safe append; no magic
  //     bump, same as Drift/Ramp were added). MIGRATION for projects saved
  //     before this field: they have no bytes here, so the read runs past EOF
  //     and REAPER leaves slot_cappoint unchanged. We detect that on READ with a
  //     sentinel dropped into slot 0 just before the read -- if it survives, the
  //     field was absent, so we seed every slot from the OLD global capture point
  //     (slider3). Pre-fix, all slots were analyzed at that one point, so this
  //     reproduces the old sound exactly. On WRITE (file_avail < 0) the sentinel
  //     is never set, so the real values are saved. ---
  file_avail(0) >= 0 ? ( slot_cappoint[0] = -999; );
  file_mem(0, slot_cappoint, NSLOTS);
  (file_avail(0) >= 0 && slot_cappoint[0] == -999) ? (
    seed_cp = slider3 * 0.01;
    scp = 0; loop(NSLOTS, slot_cappoint[scp] = seed_cp; scp += 1; );
  );

  // --- Per-slot Capture average, appended after Capture point. EOF-safe: a
  //     project saved before it existed reads past the end, keeps the sentinel,
  //     and seeds to 1 -- the single-frame analysis those projects were built
  //     on, so they sound exactly as they did. ---
  file_avail(0) >= 0 ? ( slot_capavg[0] = -999; );
  file_mem(0, slot_capavg, NSLOTS);
  // Absent field: seed every slot from the VISIBLE slider, exactly as the
  // Capture point migration above does. A project saved before this existed
  // has the slider at its default of 1, so it still reproduces the
  // single-frame analysis it was built on -- but it also means the setting
  // can be dialled in from outside without surgery on the saved blob.
  (file_avail(0) >= 0 && slot_capavg[0] == -999) ? (
    seed_ca = max(1, min(CAPAVG_MAX, slider4));
    sca = 0; loop(NSLOTS, slot_capavg[sca] = seed_ca; sca += 1; );
  );

  // --- Layer banks, appended after Capture average. EOF-safe like every append
  //     before it: a project saved before Layers existed reads past the end and
  //     keeps the @init defaults, which are "every layer off" -- exactly how
  //     those projects sound. Levels, then the Custom layers' semitones and
  //     picker positions (only N_CUSTOM of those are ever meaningful). ---
  ser_magic == 7700004 ? (
    // The one-evening layout: 11 layers (3 Custom + 8 octaves), an interval
    // picker per Custom layer, and no solo. Read it in its own shape, then
    // move the four upward octaves to where they live now -- the fifths and
    // fourths were inserted between them, so old 7..10 become new 11..14.
    // Walk DOWNWARD so a destination is never written before it is read.
    file_mem(0, lay_db_base, 11);
    file_mem(0, lay_semi,    N_CUSTOM);
    file_mem(0, ser_scratch, N_CUSTOM);   // the picker positions: read to keep the cursor aligned, then dropped
    file_var(0, last_lay_sel);
  ) : ser_magic == 7700005 ? (
    file_mem(0, lay_db_base, NLAYERS);
    file_mem(0, lay_semi,    N_CUSTOM);
    file_var(0, last_lay_sel);
  ) : (
    file_mem(0, lay_db_base, N_LAY_SLOTS);
    file_mem(0, lay_semi,    N_CUSTOM);
    file_var(0, last_lay_sel);
  );
  // Active flags APPENDED after the block above rather than slotted in beside
  // the levels they belong with. Order is the format here -- the reads are one
  // sequential cursor -- so putting them next to lay_db_base would misalign any
  // blob already written without them. Appending is EOF-safe: an older blob
  // stops here and every layer keeps its @init default of Active, which is the
  // state those projects were saved in.
  ser_magic == 7700004 ? (
    file_mem(0, lay_active, 11);
    // Migrate on READ only. On a write ser_magic is 7700005, so this is
    // unreachable there and cannot corrupt a save.
    file_avail(0) >= 0 ? (
      mig = 10;
      loop(4,
        lay_db_base[mig+4] = lay_db_base[mig];
        lay_active[mig+4]  = lay_active[mig];
        lay_db_base[mig] = -60; lay_active[mig] = 1;   // the vacated slots are the new fifths/fourths
        mig -= 1;
      );
      last_lay_sel >= 7 ? last_lay_sel += 4;
      mig = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, lay_solo[mig] = 0; mig += 1; );   // solo did not exist yet
      lay_db_base[N_LAY_SLOTS-1] = 0; lay_active[N_LAY_SLOTS-1] = 1;   // nor did the original's own slot (end-of-list; rotated below)
    );
  ) : ser_magic == 7700005 ? (
    // 15 layers and no slot for the original. Read what is there, then seed the
    // original to unity and audible -- which is what those projects sounded
    // like, since the base voice had no level of its own to save.
    file_mem(0, lay_active, NLAYERS);
    file_mem(0, lay_solo,   NLAYERS);
    file_avail(0) >= 0 ? (
      lay_db_base[N_LAY_SLOTS-1] = 0; lay_active[N_LAY_SLOTS-1] = 1; lay_solo[N_LAY_SLOTS-1] = 0;   // end-of-list; the rotate below moves it to the front
    );
  ) : (
    file_mem(0, lay_active,  N_LAY_SLOTS);
    file_mem(0, lay_solo,    N_LAY_SLOTS);
  );

  // --- Per-layer harmonic cap, appended after Solo. EOF-safe like every append
  //     before it (Drift, Ramp, Capture point/average): a project saved before
  //     this existed reads past the end here and lay_nharm keeps its @init
  //     default of 0 (uncapped) for every layer -- exactly how those projects
  //     already sound, so nothing changes on load. No magic bump needed and no
  //     entry in the layer-order permutation below: it was introduced at the
  //     current (already-canonical) layer order, so there is no older ordering
  //     for it to need migrating from. ---
  file_mem(0, lay_nharm, N_LAY_SLOTS);
  // Appended, exactly as lay_nharm itself was. A blob written before this
  // existed simply runs out here; file_mem leaves the destination untouched
  // past EOF, so the array keeps @init's -1 (follow the global) and every
  // older project sounds precisely as it did. No magic bump needed, and none
  // wanted -- a bump would make every 7700008 blob fall through to defaults.
  // NOT added to the layer-order permutation below: it is only ever RESTORED
  // from blobs already in ladder order, and migrating a DEFAULTED bank is the
  // mistake that moved the Original's unity gain off its slot last time.
  file_mem(0, lay_ot_harm, N_LAY_SLOTS);

  // --- Drift and Ramp play/rest, APPENDED last. EOF-safe exactly like every
  //     append before it: a project saved before these existed runs past the
  //     end here, file_mem leaves the banks untouched, and they keep @init's 0
  //     -- which is "always moving", precisely how those projects sound. No
  //     magic bump, and none wanted: a bump would make every existing 7700008
  //     blob fall through to defaults and lose its captures.
  //     NOT added to the layer-order permutation below, for the same reason
  //     lay_nharm is not: these are only ever restored from blobs already in
  //     ladder order, and permuting a DEFAULTED bank is the mistake that moved
  //     the Original's unity gain off its slot. Migrate what was RESTORED.
  file_mem(0, target_drift_play, n_ser_targets);
  file_mem(0, target_drift_rest, n_ser_targets);
  file_mem(0, ramp_play_mem,     n_ser_targets);
  file_mem(0, ramp_rest_mem,     n_ser_targets);

  // --- 7700087 (2026-09-13): the pitch layout's banks, APPENDED at the very end. Gated on the
  //     magic that WROTE them, never on EOF (Passage's rule). The selector is saved as SHOWN,
  //     0 = All, since last_lay_sel above holds only a layer. ---
  ser_new ? (
    file_avail(0) < 0 ? ( ser_lay_selv = last_lay_selv; );
    file_mem(0, lay_semi,  N_LAY_SLOTS);
    file_mem(0, lay_punit, N_LAY_SLOTS);
    file_mem(0, lay_fine,  N_LAY_SLOTS);
    file_mem(0, lay_funit, N_LAY_SLOTS);
    file_var(0, ser_lay_selv);
    file_mem(0, target_drift_unit, N_TARGETS);
    file_mem(0, ramp_by_unit,      N_TARGETS);
  );

  // --- Layer order migration, walked LAST so every older layout above has
  //     already been brought up to CANON: Custom 1..3, the twelve intervals low
  //     to high, then the original at the end.
  //
  //     The list is now a PITCH LADDER with the original at unison, mid-list,
  //     which is where the ear expects it. 7700007 -- which put it first -- is
  //     un-rotated back to CANON before the ladder permutation runs, so both
  //     old shapes converge on one table.
  //
  //     Every per-layer bank and the layer range of every per-target bank get
  //     the same treatment, because target LAY_T0+i has always meant layer i.
  //
  //     THE SLIDER LINE CANNOT BE FIXED FROM HERE. slider29, slider46 and
  //     slider56 hold indices into these lists and live in the project file --
  //     run tools/morpher_migrate_layer_order.py over the project folders.
  // Gated on the blob actually HAVING layer banks (7700004 was the first), not
  // merely on being old. A 7700001/7700002 blob predates layers entirely, so
  // those banks were never read and still hold the @init defaults -- which are
  // laid out in the CURRENT order already. Permuting them moved the original's
  // unity gain off its slot and dropped -60 dB in its place, so base_gain went
  // to zero and the voice AND the wash base went silent on every pre-layers
  // project. "Migrate what was restored" -- never what was defaulted.
  (file_avail(0) >= 0 && ser_magic >= 7700004 && ser_magic < 7700008) ? (
    ser_magic == 7700007 ? (
      permute_bank(lay_db_base, 0, lay_unrot);
      permute_bank(lay_active,  0, lay_unrot);
      permute_bank(lay_solo,    0, lay_unrot);
      permute_bank(target_drift_up,    LAY_T0, lay_unrot);
      permute_bank(target_drift_down,  LAY_T0, lay_unrot);
      permute_bank(target_drift_per,   LAY_T0, lay_unrot);
      permute_bank(target_drift_shape, LAY_T0, lay_unrot);
      permute_bank(ramp_by_mem,        LAY_T0, lay_unrot);
      permute_bank(ramp_dur_mem,       LAY_T0, lay_unrot);
      permute_bank(ramp_delay_mem,     LAY_T0, lay_unrot);
      last_lay_sel = last_lay_sel <= 0 ? N_LAY_SLOTS-1 : last_lay_sel - 1;
      last_target_select >= LAY_T0 ? (
        last_target_select = last_target_select <= LAY_T0 ? 23 : last_target_select - 1; );
      last_ramp_target >= LAY_T0 ? (
        last_ramp_target = last_ramp_target <= LAY_T0 ? 23 : last_ramp_target - 1; );
    );
    permute_bank(lay_db_base, 0, lay_perm);
    permute_bank(lay_active,  0, lay_perm);
    permute_bank(lay_solo,    0, lay_perm);
    permute_bank(target_drift_up,    LAY_T0, lay_perm);
    permute_bank(target_drift_down,  LAY_T0, lay_perm);
    permute_bank(target_drift_per,   LAY_T0, lay_perm);
    permute_bank(target_drift_shape, LAY_T0, lay_perm);
    permute_bank(ramp_by_mem,        LAY_T0, lay_perm);
    permute_bank(ramp_dur_mem,       LAY_T0, lay_perm);
    permute_bank(ramp_delay_mem,     LAY_T0, lay_perm);
    last_lay_sel = lay_inv[max(0, min(N_LAY_SLOTS-1, last_lay_sel))];
    last_target_select >= LAY_T0 ? (
      last_target_select = LAY_T0 + lay_inv[max(0, min(N_LAY_SLOTS-1, last_target_select - LAY_T0))]; );
    last_ramp_target >= LAY_T0 ? (
      last_ramp_target = LAY_T0 + lay_inv[max(0, min(N_LAY_SLOTS-1, last_ramp_target - LAY_T0))]; );
  );

  // --- The 24-target list -> the 55 of 2026-09-11, walked after every migration
  //     above has brought an old blob to the 24-target list. Defaults are uniform
  //     across targets, so a bank a short blob never held moves only defaults.
  (file_avail(0) >= 0 && ser_magic < 7700055) ? (
    mo_remap(target_drift_up, 0);    mo_remap(target_drift_down, 0);
    mo_remap(target_drift_per, 30);  mo_remap(target_drift_shape, 0);
    mo_remap(ramp_by_mem, 0);        mo_remap(ramp_dur_mem, 0);
    mo_remap(ramp_delay_mem, 0);
    mo_remap(target_drift_play, 0);  mo_remap(target_drift_rest, 0);
    mo_remap(ramp_play_mem, 0);      mo_remap(ramp_rest_mem, 0);
    last_target_select = mo_o2n(max(0, min(23, last_target_select | 0)));
    last_ramp_target   = mo_o2n(max(0, min(23, last_ramp_target | 0)));
  );

  // --- The 55-target list -> the 87 of 2026-09-13, walked after the remap above has brought
  //     every older blob to 55. Uses lay_ladinv, so it reads the layer entries in ladder order.
  (file_avail(0) >= 0 && ser_magic <= 7700055) ? (
    t87_remap(target_drift_up, 0);    t87_remap(target_drift_down, 0);
    t87_remap(target_drift_per, 30);  t87_remap(target_drift_shape, 0);
    t87_remap(ramp_by_mem, 0);        t87_remap(ramp_dur_mem, 0);
    t87_remap(ramp_delay_mem, 0);
    t87_remap(target_drift_play, 0);  t87_remap(target_drift_rest, 0);
    t87_remap(ramp_play_mem, 0);      t87_remap(ramp_rest_mem, 0);
    last_target_select = t55_o2n(max(0, min(54, last_target_select | 0)));
    last_ramp_target   = t55_o2n(max(0, min(54, last_ramp_target | 0)));
  );

  // --- The pitch ladder -> Layer 1-16 (2026-09-13), walked after every migration above has
  //     brought an old blob to ladder order. Gated on the blob HAVING layer banks (7700004
  //     and later): a pre-layers blob holds @init's defaults, already in the new order.
  //     lay_nharm and lay_ot_harm may be defaults too; theirs are the same for every layer.
  (file_avail(0) >= 0 && ser_magic >= 7700004 && ser_magic <= 7700055) ? (
    permute_bank(lay_db_base, 0, lay_lad);
    permute_bank(lay_active,  0, lay_lad);
    permute_bank(lay_solo,    0, lay_lad);
    permute_bank(lay_nharm,   0, lay_lad);
    permute_bank(lay_ot_harm, 0, lay_lad);
    // The three Custom pitches were read into 0-2; they are Layers 14-16 now, and the other
    // thirteen take the ladder's intervals, which is what they played.
    lay_semi[13] = lay_semi[0]; lay_semi[14] = lay_semi[1]; lay_semi[15] = lay_semi[2];
    lay_seed_ladder();
    lk = 0; loop(N_LAY_SLOTS, lay_punit[lk] = 1; lay_fine[lk] = 0; lay_funit[lk] = 2; lk += 1; );
    last_lay_sel = lay_ladinv[max(0, min(N_LAY_SLOTS-1, last_lay_sel | 0))];
  );

  // TRACK-DUPLICATE FIX (CLAUDE.md nested-selector gotcha). On READ (load /
  // duplicate) file_avail(0) >= 0; on WRITE (save / undo) it's < 0. @serialize
  // is the one section guaranteed to run on both load AND duplicate, so after
  // restoring the banks we force the visible Drift AND Ramp sliders back to the
  // restored selected targets -- correcting any transient @slider clobber and
  // restoring the "visible slider == bank[selected]" invariant. sliderchange
  // (NOT slider_automate) here -- automate would write automation on load.
  file_avail(0) >= 0 ? (
    last_target_select = max(0, min(N_TARGETS-1, last_target_select));
    last_ramp_target   = max(0, min(N_TARGETS-1, last_ramp_target));
    dts = mo_read_slot(last_target_select);
    rts = mo_read_slot(last_ramp_target);
    // The selectors themselves, so a remapped old save shows the target it had.
    slider46 = last_target_select;
    slider56 = last_ramp_target;
    slider47 = target_drift_up[dts];
    slider48 = target_drift_down[dts];
    slider50 = target_drift_per[dts];
    slider52 = target_drift_shape[dts];
    slider57 = ramp_by_mem[rts];
    slider60 = ramp_dur_mem[rts];
    slider64 = ramp_delay_mem[rts];
    slider53 = target_drift_play[dts];
    slider54 = target_drift_rest[dts];
    slider61 = ramp_play_mem[rts];
    slider62 = ramp_rest_mem[rts];
    slider49 = target_drift_unit[dts];
    slider58 = ramp_by_unit[rts];
    dui_last[11] = slider49; dui_last[12] = slider58;
    dui_last[0] = slider47; dui_last[1] = slider48; dui_last[2] = slider50;
    dui_last[3] = slider52; dui_last[4] = slider53; dui_last[5] = slider54;
    dui_last[6] = slider57; dui_last[7] = slider60; dui_last[8] = slider64;
    dui_last[9] = slider61; dui_last[10] = slider62;
    // Per-slot Capture point: force slider3 to the restored slot's stored point,
    // and sync last_cap_slot so the first @slider doesn't mistake load for a
    // slot-switch (which would save the visible point into the wrong slot).
    last_cap_sel = max(0, min(NSLOTS, floor(slider1 + 0.5)));   // 0 = All, 1-8 = Slot 1-8
    last_cap_all = last_cap_sel == 0;
    cps = last_cap_all ? 0 : last_cap_sel - 1;   // engine is 0-7; All shows Slot 1
    last_cap_slot = cps;
    slider3 = slot_cappoint[cps] * 100;
    cp_ui_last = slider3;
    // Capture average is GLOBAL now, so this stamps the visible slider onto every
    // slot rather than loading one slot's value into the slider. A project saved
    // back when it was per slot flattens to the value the slider shows -- which
    // is the one its owner sees, and now the one they hear. @slider stamps too,
    // so it converges whichever of the two restore paths REAPER runs first.
    sca3 = max(1, min(CAPAVG_MAX, slider4));
    sca4 = 0; loop(NSLOTS, slot_capavg[sca4] = sca3; sca4 += 1; );
    // Layer interval pickers: ADOPT the restored positions, never fire on them.
    // The guard in @slider covers the ordinary load, but @serialize's read
    // branch is the one place guaranteed to run on both load AND track
    // duplicate, so syncing here closes the case where an early @slider ran
    // against pre-restore values. Firing on load would call slider_automate
    // during project open -- writing into an automation lane nobody touched.
    // Layer selector: same treatment as Drift/Ramp above. Force the visible
    // sliders back to the restored selected layer and sync last_lay_sel, so the
    // first @slider can't mistake a load for a selector switch and save the
    // wrong layer's values over the right one's. This is the section that runs
    // on a track DUPLICATE, where @init may not run at all -- without it the
    // selected layer is the one that silently zeroes.
    // Index off the BLOB's own last_lay_sel, NEVER off slider29 -- this is the
    // whole point of doing it here. Reading the slider assumes REAPER has
    // already restored the slider line, and the two restore paths have no
    // guaranteed order: if the slider is still at its default the visible
    // controls get loaded with the WRONG layer's values, and then the real
    // selector arrives, @slider reads it as a selector CHANGE, and the save
    // branch writes those wrong values into a different layer's bank slot.
    // The bank is scrambled and the next save persists the scramble.
    // Overwriting last_lay_sel from the slider made it worse by destroying the
    // one correct copy of the index we had just read back.
    // (The Capture-slot block below DOES read its slider -- correctly, because
    // last_cap_slot is not in the stream, so the slider is its only source.)
    // Layers (2026-09-13): the selector as the blob last showed it, and its nine controls.
    last_lay_sel = max(0, min(N_LAY_SLOTS-1, last_lay_sel | 0));
    lay_selv = ser_new ? max(0, min(N_LAY_SLOTS, ser_lay_selv | 0)) : last_lay_sel + 1;
    last_lay_sel = lay_selv == 0 ? 0 : lay_selv - 1; last_lay_selv = lay_selv;
    slider29 = lay_selv;
    slider30 = lay_active[last_lay_sel];
    slider31 = lay_semi[last_lay_sel];
    slider32 = lay_punit[last_lay_sel];
    slider33 = lay_fine[last_lay_sel];
    slider34 = lay_funit[last_lay_sel];
    slider35 = lay_db_base[last_lay_sel];
    slider36 = lay_solo[last_lay_sel];
    slider37 = lay_nharm[last_lay_sel];
    slider38 = lay_ot_harm[last_lay_sel];
    lay_adopt(); lay_ui_inited = 1;
    sliderchange(-1);
  );
);
